{ id:'enthd-thr-599', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-neck',
  stem:'Which of the following accurately describes **embryogenesis of the neck**?',
  options:['Pharynx separates head from heart by 5th intrauterine week','A constriction appears between head and thorax by 7th intrauterine week','True neck is found by 12th intrauterine week','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All three dates are printed on one slide, in this order, so the question is a straight transcription of it.** `L5) NECK MASS.pdf`, *Embryogenesis of the neck*:\n\n> "**Adult Neck does not exist in Embryo** · **5th week, pharynx separates head from heart** · **7th week, constriction between head & thorax** · **12th week, true neck is visible**."\n\n| Option | Slide wording | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| a. Pharynx separates head from heart, 5th week | "5th week, pharynx separates head from heart" | ✔ verbatim |\n| b. Constriction between head and thorax, 7th week | "7th week, constriction between head & thorax" | ✔ verbatim |\n| c. True neck found by 12th week | "12th week, true neck is visible" | ✔ verbatim |\n| d. **All of the above** | — | ✅ **the key** |\n| e. None of the above | — | ❌ fails on three counts |\n\n**Why the sequence is worth holding rather than the three dates separately.** The neck is not an organ that grows; it is a **space that opens up** between two structures that begin fused. First the pharynx pushes the heart away from the head, then the body wall constricts between head and thorax, and only then is there anything that can be called a neck. `L5` adds what fills it: "the appearance of neck structures depends on differentiation & migration of organs within the neck mainly: **Foregut & Branchial Apparatus**" — which is why every congenital neck mass in the deck traces back to one of those two, thyroglossal to the foregut-derived thyroid tract and branchial anomalies to the arches.\n\n**⚠️ Grade Gain prints the same slide inverted.** `entqb-thr6-303` asks which statement is **NOT** true and keys *"Adult Neck exists in embryo"* — the deck’s opening line, negated. Between the two questions the whole slide is examinable. `entqb-thr6-306` takes the second half and keys the **branchial apparatus** as the source of the lateral neck structures.\n\n**Bottom line: 5 – 7 – 12, and no neck before 12 weeks.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The neck forms in three printed steps — pharynx separating head from heart at 5 weeks, a head–thorax constriction at 7, a true neck visible at 12 — from foregut and branchial apparatus derivatives.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.153 (Throat ch.5, Neck masses — the chapter heading prints mid-page immediately above Q598). All three dates quoted verbatim from the *Embryogenesis of the neck* slide of `L5) NECK MASS.pdf`. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-303` (same slide, negated stem), `entqb-thr6-306`. Five options, A–E, as printed.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-601', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-neck',
  stem:'All of the following are **midline neck masses** **except**?',
  options:['Thyroglossal duct cyst','Enlarged lymph node','Dermoid cyst','Branchial cyst'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**A branchial cyst is the one lesion on this list that is defined by a LATERAL position, and the deck says so in the same breath as it names it.** `L5) NECK MASS.pdf`, under *1- Congenital : B- Branchial*:\n\n> **2nd Branchial cyst:** "**Commonest, upper neck, ANTERIOR TO SCM.** full track may reach the tonsil."\n\nAnterior to sternomastoid is the anterior triangle, not the midline — and it is the **second** cleft that accounts for almost all of them (`entqb-thr6-308` keys exactly that).\n\n**Why the other three can sit in the midline:**\n\n| Option | `L5` / other-bank grounding |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Thyroglossal duct cyst** | "Anywhere from **foramen cecum to neck** · **90% in midline, 85% below hyoid**" — the tract descends down the midline from the tongue base, which is also why it **moves on tongue protrusion** (`entqb-thr6-307`). The single most midline mass in the neck |\n| **b. Enlarged lymph node** | the deck files lymphadenitis and metastatic nodes under *4- Inflammatory* and *7- Malignant · Metastatic* without giving a site, but **submental and pretracheal nodes lie in the midline**, and a node is the commonest neck swelling overall — the bank keys that at its own Q608 |\n| **c. Dermoid cyst** | `L5` lists it among the six congenital masses ("**E- Dermoid**") without printing its site. **The other bank supplies it**: `entqb-thr6-304` offers dermoid cyst as a distractor on *commonest **midline** congenital neck mass in children*, and `entep-throat-50` does the same for *most common **midline infrahyoid** neck lesion* — both option lists put dermoid in the midline group and key thyroglossal above it. **That is the course’s own second bank, not outside knowledge** |\n\n**⚠️ The bank asks this question twice over one option list, three pages apart, and the odd-one-out moves.** Its Q604 keeps branchial cyst and asks which is **not congenital** instead, where the answer becomes **Zenker’s diverticulum** — see `enthd-thr-604`. Read the qualifier before the list.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Midline in a child = thyroglossal until proven otherwise; **lateral, upper, in front of sternomastoid, in a young adult = branchial**. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-308`, `entqb-thr6-309`, `entqb-thr6-304`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A branchial cyst is lateral — upper neck, anterior to sternomastoid — whereas thyroglossal cyst, dermoid and enlarged nodes can all present in the midline.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.154 (Throat ch.5, Neck masses). Branchial site and thyroglossal 90 %-midline figure quoted verbatim from `L5) NECK MASS.pdf`. **⚠️ `L5` DOES NOT PRINT THE SITE OF A DERMOID CYST — it lists "E- Dermoid" among the six congenital masses and nothing more; the midline placement is taken from the other banks’ own printed option lists (`entqb-thr6-304`, `entep-throat-50`), not from outside knowledge.** Pairs with `enthd-thr-604`, which reuses this mass list under a different qualifier. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-307`, `entqb-thr6-308`, `entqb-thr6-309`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-604', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-neck',
  stem:'All of the following masses are **congenital in nature** **except**?',
  options:['Branchial cyst','Cystic hygroma','Zenker’s diverticulum','Dermoid'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**`L5) NECK MASS.pdf` answers this by the shape of its own classification: it prints CONGENITAL and DEVELOPMENTAL as two separate groups, and Zenker’s diverticulum is in the second one.**\n\n> **Classification:** "1- **Congenital** · 2- **Developmental** · 3- Normal structures · 4- Traumatic · 5- Inflammatory · 6- Deep Neck Space Infection · 7- Benign Tumors · 8- Malignant Tumors"\n>\n> **1- Congenital:** "A- **Thyroglossal** · B- **Branchial** · C- Thymus · D- Haemangioma · E- **Dermoid** · F- **Cystic Hygroma**"\n>\n> **2- Developmental:** "A- **Laryngocele** · B- **Zenker’s Diverticulum**"\n\nThree of the four options are printed in the congenital list. The fourth is printed in the developmental list. **The question is testing whether the two lists were read as two lists.**\n\n**What actually makes Zenker’s different.** `L10) Dysphagia From ENT Perspective.pdf` gives the mechanism: it is "an **outpouching of mucosa and submucosa through the Killian triangle**, it as a **false diverticulum**", developing "between a weakness in the inferior constrictors (**thyropharyngeus/cricopharyngeus**), more commonly on the **left**", possibly because "the **cricopharyngeal sphincter may have a higher than normal resting tone**", and — the decisive clause — "**Frequently, it is elderly patients**". A herniation driven through a muscular gap by years of abnormal pressure is **acquired**; nothing about it is present at birth.\n\n| Option | Group in `L5` | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| a. Branchial cyst | Congenital | "Commonest, upper neck, anterior to SCM" |\n| b. Cystic hygroma | Congenital | listed as "F- Cystic Hygroma"; `entqb-thr6-310` keys that it has **no distinct border** |\n| **c. Zenker’s diverticulum** | **Developmental** | ✅ **the key** — a false, pulsion diverticulum of the elderly |\n| d. Dermoid | Congenital | listed as "E- Dermoid" |\n\n**⚠️ Zenker’s and laryngocele are the deck’s only two "developmental" masses and it presents BOTH as lateral neck swellings** — "Lateral neck mass, gurgling sensation, aspiration" for Zenker’s, "Lateral compressible neck mass at level of hyoid" for the laryngocele. `entqb-thr6-312` keys **halitosis** as the true statement about Zenker’s and offers *"It is a midline neck mass"* as a distractor; `entqb-thr6-311` does the same job for the laryngocele. **The pair is easy to confuse and the bank exploits it.**\n\n**Bottom line: present from birth = congenital; herniated through a weak spot over decades = developmental/acquired.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'`L5` splits neck masses into CONGENITAL (thyroglossal, branchial, thymus, haemangioma, dermoid, cystic hygroma) and DEVELOPMENTAL (laryngocele, Zenker’s diverticulum) — Zenker’s is an acquired pulsion diverticulum of the elderly, not a congenital mass.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.154 (Throat ch.5, Neck masses). Both classification lists quoted verbatim from `L5) NECK MASS.pdf`; the Killian-triangle mechanism, the false-diverticulum point, the left-sided predilection and the elderly age group from `L10) Dysphagia From ENT Perspective.pdf`. Straddles `ent-dysph` by mechanism; filed under `ent-neck` because the question is asked from the neck-mass classification. Pairs with `enthd-thr-601` on the same mass list. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-312`, `entqb-thr6-311`, `entqb-thr6-310`. The bank prints the curly apostrophe in *Zenker’s*; reproduced.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-605', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-neck',
  stem:'A **48-year-old male** visits the family doctor with a **left-sided neck swelling that appeared 5 months ago and increased gradually in size**. What is the **initial step in management** of such a patient?',
  options:['Assurance','Excision biopsy','Incision biopsy','CT neck'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Image it. `L5) NECK MASS.pdf` puts imaging first on its own investigation list, and everything about this vignette says the mass must be assumed malignant until it is proved otherwise.**\n\n> "Evidence suggests that a **neck mass in the adult patient should be considered MALIGNANT UNTIL PROVEN OTHERWISE**."\n>\n> **Rule of 7:** "Swelling existed for 7 days: **Inflammatory** · Swelling existed for 7 **months**: **Neoplastic** · Swelling existed for 7 **years**: Congenital/Developmental."\n>\n> **80:20 Rule:** "**Adult Neck Mass = 80 : 20 malignant : Benign.**"\n>\n> **III- Investigations:** "1- **Imaging** · 2- F.N.A. · 3- Endoscopy for 1ry. · 4- Aspiration for C&S · 5- **Excision biopsy** · 6- Tumor markers · 7- Abdominal imaging · 8- Skin Test."\n\nA 48-year-old man is in `L5`’s "Late Adult 40+" band, whose listed differential begins with **Malignancy**; five months of painless gradual enlargement sits on the neoplastic side of the Rule of 7; and the mass is **lateral**, which `L5` singles out — "an asymptomatic **lateral** neck mass in an adult may be the **initial or only** clinically apparent manifestation of head and neck cancer, such as squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), lymphoma, thyroid, or salivary gland cancer."\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, in ascending order of harm:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Assurance** | it is the exact opposite of the deck’s standing instruction. Reassuring an adult with a five-month lateral neck mass is how a curable node becomes an incurable one — `L5`’s ILOs name the goal as "reduce **delay in diagnosis** of malignant disease" |\n| **b. Excision biopsy** | **fifth** on the investigation list, not first. Excising an undiagnosed metastatic node before imaging and cytology **violates the neck planes and compromises a subsequent neck dissection**, and it is done only after FNAC has failed to give an answer — typically for suspected lymphoma, which needs architecture |\n| **c. Incision biopsy** | ⚠️ **the deck forbids it outright.** Work-up for a suspected metastatic L.N.: "**NEVER : incision biopsy**", followed by "F.N.A.C. : 1-Benign/Malignant · 2-Differentiates Lymphoma/Metastatic SCC · **3-97% sensitivity & 98% specificity**". An open incision seeds tumour into the wound and the soft tissues |\n\n**⚠️ Note what the option list leaves out.** `L5` calls the most readily available and informative investigation for most neck masses **ultrasound** (`entqb-thr6-319` keys US on a list where CT is offered), and it puts **FNAC** immediately after imaging with a 97 %/98 % accuracy. Neither is offered here, so CT is the only imaging on the menu — and contrast-enhanced CT is what the other bank keys on the identical vignette shape: `entqb-thr6-302` (50-year-old, 7-month hard enlarging mass → **CT**) and `entqb-thr6-327` (56-year-old, 9-month lateral mass → **CT with contrast**). **This bank asks the same thing at its own Q598 with *Contrast-enhanced CT* as the key.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line: image, then aspirate, and never cut into an undiagnosed neck node.** Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-302`, `entqb-thr6-327`, `entqb-thr6-319`, `entqb-thr6-301`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'An adult neck mass is malignant until proven otherwise; the work-up begins with imaging (then FNAC, 97 %/98 % accurate), excision biopsy comes fifth, and incision biopsy is explicitly forbidden.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.154 (Throat ch.5, Neck masses). Malignant-until-proven-otherwise statement, Rule of 7, 80:20 rule, age-group differential, the eight-item investigation list and the "NEVER : incision biopsy" instruction all quoted verbatim from `L5) NECK MASS.pdf`. **The tumour-seeding and neck-plane arguments behind options b and c are the reasoning behind the deck’s prohibition and are not printed on the slide** *(not taken from the course material)*. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-302`, `entqb-thr6-327`, `entqb-thr6-319`, `entqb-thr6-301`. The bank prints a near-identical vignette at its own Q598 keying *Contrast-enhanced CT*; that item is outside this batch.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-606', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-neck',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common congenital neck swelling**?',
  options:['Branchial cyst','Thyroglossal cyst','Dermoid cyst','Hemangioma'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Thyroglossal cyst — `L5) NECK MASS.pdf` gives it the ranking in its opening line, in the plural, as printed:**\n\n> **1- Congenital : A- Thyroglossal** — "**Commonest congenital neck swellings** · Anywhere from **foramen cecum to neck** · **90% in midline, 85% below hyoid** · Solid mass, cyst, or sinus."\n\nThe tract is the remnant of the thyroid’s descent from the **foramen caecum** at the tongue base to its final position, which is why the lesion is midline, why it can be anywhere along that line, and why it **rises on tongue protrusion** — the fact `entqb-thr6-307` keys as true.\n\n**Why the other three rank below it:**\n\n| Option | `L5` | Why not commonest |\n|---|---|---|\n| **a. Branchial cyst** | "**2nd Branchial cyst: Commonest**, upper neck, anterior to SCM" | commonest **branchial** anomaly, not commonest congenital neck swelling — and lateral, not midline (`entqb-thr6-308`) |\n| **c. Dermoid cyst** | listed only as "**E- Dermoid**" among the six congenital masses | the deck attaches no figure to it; the other bank offers it as a distractor against thyroglossal twice and keys thyroglossal both times (`entqb-thr6-304`, `entep-throat-50`) |\n| **d. Hemangioma** | listed as "**D- Haemangioma**"; `entqb-thr6-310` keys that **CT is not mandatory** for it | a vascular lesion, and in this deck a distractor rather than a contender |\n\n**⚠️ THIS IS ONE OF FOUR "MOST COMMON NECK SWELLING" QUESTIONS PRINTED IN A ROW OVER NEARLY THE SAME OPTION BLOCK, AND EACH QUALIFIER MOVES THE KEY.** The bank prints, in order: most common **congenital** → thyroglossal cyst (this question) · most common **midline cystic** → thyroglossal cyst · most common (**unqualified**) → **lymph node enlargement** · most common **lateral cystic** → **branchial cyst** (`enthd-thr-609`). **Two of the four sit outside this batch.** Read the adjective before the noun: *congenital*, *midline*, *lateral* and *unqualified* are four different questions.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A midline cystic swelling in a child that moves with the tongue is a thyroglossal cyst, and it is excised **with its tract and the body of the hyoid** — Sistrunk’s operation — because the tract is what recurs. `entqb-thr6-307` keys *"If infected, perform incision and drainage"* as the **false** statement: draining it converts a cyst into a fistula. *(Sistrunk’s name is **not taken from the course material**; `L5` prints only "It is removed with its tract" through the other bank.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Thyroglossal cyst is the commonest congenital neck swelling — 90 % midline, 85 % below the hyoid, anywhere from foramen caecum down — and it is excised with its tract, never incised and drained.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.155 (Throat ch.5, Neck masses). "Commonest congenital neck swellings", the foramen-caecum range and the 90 %/85 % figures quoted verbatim from `L5) NECK MASS.pdf`. **⚠️ MEMBER OF A DELIBERATE FOUR-QUESTION GRADED SET (Q606–Q609) over near-identical option blocks with three different keys; Q607 and Q608 folded into corpus entries and are not in this batch — DO NOT MERGE THIS WITH `enthd-thr-609`.** The eponym Sistrunk is tagged inline as not taken from the course material. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-307`, `entqb-thr6-304`, `entqb-thr6-308`, `entep-throat-50`, `enthd-thr-609`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-609', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-neck',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common lateral cystic neck swelling**?',
  options:['Lymph node','Branchial cyst','Cystic hygroma','Laryngocele'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Branchial cyst — and the word doing the work is CYSTIC.** `L5) NECK MASS.pdf` names it and places it in one line:\n\n> **2nd Branchial cyst:** "**Commonest**, upper neck, **anterior to SCM**. full track may reach the tonsil."\n\nUpper neck, in front of sternomastoid, fluid-filled, classically presenting in a young adult — that is the commonest cystic swelling of the lateral neck, and `entqb-thr6-308` keys the **second** cleft as the commonest branchial pathology of any kind.\n\n**Why each of the other three fails on a different word of the stem:**\n\n| Option | What it is in `L5` | Which word it fails |\n|---|---|---|\n| **a. Lymph node** | filed under *4- Inflammatory · lymphadenitis* and *7- Malignant · Metastatic*; the commonest neck swelling **of all**, which is what the bank keys at its own Q608 | **cystic** — a node is a **solid** swelling. It wins the unqualified question and loses this one |\n| **c. Cystic hygroma** | "F- **Cystic Hygroma**" among the six congenital masses; `entqb-thr6-310` keys that it has **no distinct border** | **commonest** — genuinely lateral and genuinely cystic, but far rarer, and it is a soft, ill-defined, transilluminating lymphatic malformation of infancy rather than a discrete cyst |\n| **d. Laryngocele** | *2- Developmental : A- Laryngocele* — "**Air-filled** cystic dilatation of laryngeal saccule · External – Internal – Mixed · **Lateral compressible neck mass at level of hyoid**" | **commonest**, and arguably *cystic* — the deck calls it air-filled, so it is a compressible swelling that empties, not a fluid cyst; `entqb-thr6-311` keys that it is **not** low in the neck |\n\n**⚠️ Every distractor here is the right answer to a neighbouring question, which is the whole design of this run of items.** Lymph node answers *most common neck swelling*; thyroglossal cyst answers *most common congenital* and *most common midline cystic* (`enthd-thr-606`); branchial cyst answers *most common lateral cystic*. **The four are printed consecutively over near-identical option blocks and must never be merged.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A smooth fluctuant swelling at the upper third of the anterior border of sternomastoid in a patient in their twenties is a branchial cyst — but in an adult over 40 the same swelling is a **cystic metastatic node until proven otherwise**, which is why `L5`’s standing rule ("**malignant until proven otherwise**") outranks the epidemiology at the bedside. *(The age caveat is **not taken from the course material**.)* Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-308`, `entqb-thr6-309`, `entqb-thr6-311`, `entqb-thr6-310`, `enthd-thr-606`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The commonest lateral CYSTIC neck swelling is the second branchial cleft cyst — upper neck, anterior to sternomastoid; a lymph node is commoner but solid, and cystic hygroma and laryngocele are far rarer.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.155 (Throat ch.5, Neck masses). Branchial, laryngocele and cystic-hygroma entries quoted verbatim from `L5) NECK MASS.pdf`. **⚠️ MEMBER OF THE Q606–Q609 GRADED SET — Q607 and Q608 folded and are outside this batch; DO NOT MERGE WITH `enthd-thr-606`.** **⚠️ `L5` never states that a lymph node is solid or that a branchial cyst presents in young adults; both are tagged inline as outside the course material, as is the over-40 cystic-metastasis caveat.** Cross-reference: `entqb-thr6-308`, `entqb-thr6-309`, `entqb-thr6-310`, `entqb-thr6-311`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-616', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'A **25-year-old patient attended a football match** and after the match he complained of **hoarseness of voice in the right vocal cord**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vocal cord polyp','Vocal cord nodule','Contact granuloma','Intubation granuloma'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**One episode of violent shouting, one cord affected — that is a vocal fold polyp, and unilaterality is the discriminator the stem hands you.** `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`:\n\n> **VOCAL CORD POLYPS** — "very common **benign non neoplastic tumors** of the vocal cord, it is mainly a disease of **males**, most patients are **smokers**, many **use their voice professionally**. The age peak is between **30 and 50** years."\n>\n> **Types of polyps:** "**Hemorrhagic** · Hyalinous · Fibrous · gelatinous."\n>\n> **Signs:** "small **pedunculated or sessile** lesions · on the **anterior third of the free edge** of the true vocal cord · They are **mobile during phonation** when pedunculated · **Polyps occur mostly SINGULARLY and UNILATERALLY**, but bilateral cases have also been recorded."\n\nThe **haemorrhagic** type is the one that follows a single episode of vocal trauma: a submucosal bleed into the free edge of one cord after shouting, coughing or screaming. **Acute event → one cord → polyp.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each is defined by a different pattern or a different history:**\n\n| Option | `L2` | Why it fails here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **b. Vocal cord nodule** | "found more in **women, children, professional singers, lecturers** · the most common site is at the **junction of the anterior and middle thirds**, **usually BILATERAL, SYMMETRICAL** · **Immobile** during phonation" | nodules are a **chronic** callus of repeated abuse and they are **bilateral**. This stem gives one cord and one afternoon — the bank asks the nodule version at its own Q617 (`enthd-thr-617`) |\n| **c. Contact granuloma** | "**lobulated swelling on the VOCAL PROCESS of the arytenoid cartilage**, usually pale, pedunculated · more in **males** · superficial ulcers occur when the thin mucosa… is **crushed repetitively** against the opposite side" | **posterior**, not on the membranous free edge, and it needs **repetitive** crushing or reflux, not a single match |\n| **d. Intubation granuloma** | "The cause… is **endotracheal intubation**. It appears **only in adults**. The incidence is **higher in women 4 to 1**… **seen on the vocal process** of the arytenoid" | there is **no intubation** anywhere in the history, and the site is again posterior |\n\n**⚠️ Q616, Q617 and Q618 are printed as a set over one identical option block, and the three vignettes differ only in the pattern of the lesion** — one cord after an acute strain, both cords in a chronic voice user, and posterior masses in a neglected refluxer. **Read *right* against *right and left* before anything else.**\n\n**Treatment, since it separates polyp from nodule too.** `L2` on polyps: "Small polyps caused by irritating factors such as **smoking may be treated with smoking cessation alone**. Other which are large in size the Therapy is **surgical**… **suspension microlaryngoscopic surgery**… both followed by **2 days of complete voice rest**." On nodules: "**speech therapy**, in case of failure, surgical removal… **indicated only when it become mature**." **A polyp is cut; a nodule is talked down first.**\n\nCross-reference: `enthd-thr-617`, `enthd-thr-618`, `entqb-thr10-507`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A haemorrhagic vocal fold polyp follows a single episode of vocal trauma and is unilateral and on the anterior third of the free edge, against the bilateral symmetrical nodule and the posteriorly sited contact and intubation granulomas.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.157 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — the chapter heading prints mid-page immediately above this question). All four lesions quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`. **⚠️ FIGURE-DEPENDENT-BY-LAYOUT, NO CROP CUT YET: the bank prints a colour endoscopic laryngeal photograph in its rounded blue frame to the right of this stem, unlabelled and unarrowed. NO `image` FIELD IS SET. The photograph shows the finding that IS the answer, so any future `imgAlt` must give modality and view ONLY — naming the lesion would answer the question.** The printed text is nonetheless sufficient: the stem states *right vocal cord*, and that unilaterality is the discriminator. **⚠️ `L2` NAMES THE HAEMORRHAGIC SUBTYPE AND NAMES PROFESSIONAL VOICE USE BUT NEVER JOINS THEM TO A SINGLE ACUTE STRAIN — the link is the OTHER BANK’S printed statement, not outside knowledge: `entqb-thr10-505` prints "Acute screaming usually causes polyps" as a TRUE option and keys a different one.** Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-617`, `enthd-thr-618`, `entqb-thr10-505`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-617', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'A **35-year-old female teacher** complains of **hoarseness of voice in right and left vocal cord**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vocal cord polyp','Vocal cord nodule','Contact granuloma','Intubation granuloma'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Vocal nodules — a female professional voice user with BOTH cords affected is `L2`’s description of the lesion almost word for word.** `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`:\n\n> **Vocal Nodules** — "found more in **women, children, professional singers, LECTURERS** · **hoareseness** · the most common site is at the **junction of the anterior and middle thirds**, **usually BILATERAL, SYMMETRICAL** · **Immobile during phonation**."\n>\n> **Symptoms:** "The voice is frequently **hoarse, breathy (with a waste of air) and easily FATIGUED**. The nodules are **smaller in singers than in non singers**, probably because a change in maximum vocal performance is a reason for a singer to note problems at an earlier stage."\n\nA teacher is the deck’s own example of the at-risk occupation, and **bilateral** is the word that closes the question. The lesion is a symmetrical callus at the point of maximum impact — the two cords strike each other hardest at the junction of the anterior and middle thirds, so that is where the nodules form, one opposite the other.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Vocal cord polyp** | `L2`: "occur **mostly singularly and unilaterally**", on the **anterior third**, "**mobile** during phonation when pedunculated", "**mainly a disease of males**, most patients are **smokers**", age peak **30–50**. Everything about this vignette — female, bilateral, chronic occupational strain — points the other way. The bank asks the polyp version at its own Q616 (`enthd-thr-616`) |\n| **c. Contact granuloma** | on the **vocal process of the arytenoid**, i.e. **posterior**; "more in **males**"; needs repetitive crushing of the mucosa over the cartilage or reflux (`enthd-thr-618`) |\n| **d. Intubation granuloma** | requires **endotracheal intubation**, which is absent from the history; also posterior, on the vocal process |\n\n**⚠️ Nodules are the one lesion here whose FIRST-LINE TREATMENT IS NOT SURGERY, and the bank tests that separately.** `L2`: "**speech therapy**, in case of failure, surgical removal by direct laryngoscopy and microlaryngosurgery is **indicated only when it become mature**." A young nodule is soft and reversible; operating on it early removes normal cord as well. **Voice therapy first, and only a mature nodule is cut.**\n\n**And note where nodules sit in the wider classification.** `L8) Introduction to phoniatrics.pdf` files "**vocal fold nodules**" at the head of the **minimal associated pathological lesions** column, beside polyp, cyst, Reinke’s oedema and contact granuloma — the group the bank asks about at `enthd-thr-646`. `entqb-thr10-507` keys **vocal nodule** as the commonest cause of hoarseness in **children**, for the same reason: shouting.\n\nCross-reference: `enthd-thr-616`, `enthd-thr-618`, `enthd-thr-646`, `entqb-thr10-507`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Vocal nodules are bilateral, symmetrical, immobile lesions at the junction of the anterior and middle thirds of the cords in women, children and professional voice users, and are treated with voice therapy before surgery.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.157 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). Nodule, polyp and granuloma descriptions quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`, including its own spellings *hoareseness* and *bilateral,symmetrical*; the MAP classification column from `L8) Introduction to phoniatrics.pdf`. **⚠️ FIGURE-DEPENDENT-BY-LAYOUT, NO CROP CUT YET: a colour endoscopic laryngeal photograph is printed in the rounded blue frame to the right of the stem, unlabelled and unarrowed. NO `image` FIELD IS SET, and any future `imgAlt` must give modality and view ONLY — Q616, Q617 and Q618 differ EXACTLY by what their photographs show, so a descriptive alt text would answer all three.** The stem alone is sufficient here: it states *right and left vocal cord*. **⚠️ The mechanical explanation for the mid-cord site is not on the slide** *(not taken from the course material)*. Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-616`, `enthd-thr-618`, `enthd-thr-646`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-618', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'A **45-year-old female diagnosed with GERD for 2 years, but she neglected the medical treatment**. She noticed a **gradual change in her voice tone**. **Laryngoscopy revealed masses in posterior 2/3 of vocal cords**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vocal cord polyp','Vocal cord nodule','Contact granuloma','Intubation granuloma'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Two years of untreated reflux plus posterior cord masses is a contact granuloma — the site and the cause are both printed.** `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`:\n\n> **Contact Granulomas** — "lobulated swelling on the **VOCAL PROCESS OF THE ARYTENOID CARTILAGE**, usually **pale, pedunculated masses** · **unilateral or bilateral** · more in **males** · superficial **ulcers** occur when the thin mucosa overlying the firm cartilage of the vocal process is **crushed repetitively** against the opposite side, causing a breakdown of the mucosa."\n>\n> **Symptoms:** "Chronic or acute **hoarseness**, **vocal fatigue, throat pain**, a rough **foreign body sensation** and **cough**."\n>\n> **Treatment:** "Resting the vocal cords if forceful speaking is the primary cause · Surgery is also done to remove the ulcers, but this is often considered a **last resort as recurrence is common** · **In cases where contact granuloma is caused by GASTROESOPHAGEAL REFLUX DISEASE, treatment for it is often initiated**."\n\nThe vocal process of the arytenoid is at the **back** of the glottis, which is exactly where the stem puts the masses — and `L2`’s reflux slide explains why it is the back: "Erythema, edema, copple stoning, **INTERARYTENOID PREDILECTION**, ulcers, **granulations** … **Causes most of MAP lesions**." **Refluxate pools posteriorly, so reflux disease is a posterior laryngeal disease.**\n\n**⚠️ THE HARD DISTRACTOR IS (d), AND ONE WORD SEPARATES THEM.** Intubation granuloma sits on **the same anatomical spot** and would also be "bilateral masses on the vocal processes":\n\n> "The cause of Intubation Granuloma is **endotracheal intubation**. It appears **only in adults**. The incidence is **higher in women 4 to 1** because the tube falls more to the **posterior commissure** and because the mucosa is thinner. It seen on the **vocal process of the arytenoid**."\n\n**A 45-year-old woman fits that demographic perfectly** — the discriminator is **not** the site, not the age and not the sex, but the **history**: there is no intubation anywhere in this vignette and there is two years of neglected reflux. **Read the history, because the anatomy will not separate these two.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Vocal cord polyp** | `L2` puts polyps on the "**anterior third** of the free edge", "mostly **singularly and unilaterally**", "mainly a disease of **males**", peak 30–50 — wrong end of the cord |\n| **b. Vocal cord nodule** | at the "**junction of the anterior and middle thirds**", **bilateral and symmetrical**, in voice abusers — again anterior, and driven by voice use rather than acid |\n| **c. Contact granuloma — the key** | ✅ posterior, reflux-driven, and `L2` prints the reflux link explicitly |\n| **d. Intubation granuloma** | right site, right demographic, **no intubation in the history** |\n\n**⚠️ The bank prints "posterior 2/3 of vocal cords".** The vocal process occupies roughly the **posterior third** of the glottic length, so the printed fraction is loose; it is transcribed as printed and it does not change the answer, since every alternative on the list is an **anterior** lesion or needs a tube. **A defective detail is recorded, never corrected.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Treat the reflux first — `L2` calls anti-reflux treatment "**mandatory**" and reserves surgery as a last resort because the granuloma recurs. `entqb-thr9-467` keys contact granuloma as the MAP lesion associated with **chronic cough**, the other half of the same reflux picture. Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-616`, `enthd-thr-617`, `entqb-thr9-467`, `entqb-thr9-465`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Contact granuloma sits on the vocal process of the arytenoid — the posterior glottis — and is driven by reflux or forceful speaking; it is separated from the identically sited intubation granuloma by the history alone.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.157–158 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). **⚠️ THE QUESTION STRADDLES THE PAGE BREAK IN THE SOURCE: stem and options A–C print at the foot of book p.157, option D and the key `Answer: C` at the head of p.158; transcribed from the printed lines, nothing inferred.** Contact granuloma, intubation granuloma and the GERD slide quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`. **⚠️ FIGURE-DEPENDENT-BY-LAYOUT, NO CROP CUT YET: a colour endoscopic laryngeal photograph is printed in the rounded blue frame to the right of the stem, unlabelled and unarrowed. NO `image` FIELD IS SET; any future `imgAlt` must give modality and view ONLY, since Q616/Q617/Q618 differ exactly by what their photographs show.** The stem is self-sufficient — it states both the reflux history and the posterior site. **⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT RECORDED, NOT CORRECTED: the bank prints "posterior 2/3 of vocal cords" where the vocal process occupies about the posterior third; the answer is unaffected.** Cross-reference: `entqb-thr9-467`, `entqb-thr9-465`, `enthd-thr-616`, `enthd-thr-617`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-621', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'**Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis in a child** is characterized by all of the following **except**?',
  options:['Frequently recurrent','Affecting multiple sites','More potentially malignant than that occurring in adults','Best treated by repeated laser excision'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The malignant potential runs the other way: the JUVENILE form recurs more and spreads more, but the ADULT form is the one that transforms.** This is not outside knowledge — **the course’s own second bank prints it twice, from both ends:**\n\n- `entqb-thr10-524` asks the mirror question — *recurrent respiratory papillomatosis **in adults** is characterized by which of the following?* — over **this identical four-option block**, and keys "**More potentially malignant than that occurring in children**".\n- `entqb-thr10-519` asks *all of the following are true **except*** and keys "**Juvenile type is more liable for malignant transformation**" as the false statement.\n\n**Two questions, opposite framing, same fact. The bank is unambiguous.**\n\n**What `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` gives directly:**\n\n> **Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis** — "**HPV 6, 11** · **Most common benign tumor of larynx** · **Infant, children** · **Spontaneous involution at puberty** · **Avoid tracheostomy** · **Laser**, debrider, **mitomycine**, valacyclovir, indol-3-carbinol, isotrentoic acid."\n\n**⚠️ "Spontaneous involution at puberty" is the slide’s own statement of the juvenile prognosis, and it is incompatible with the juvenile form being the more dangerous one.** A disease that disappears when the child grows up is not the one that turns into carcinoma.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Grounding |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Frequently recurrent** | it is the first word of the name. `entqb-thr10-545` keys **surgical excision** as first-line precisely because the lesion returns and must be cleared again and again |\n| **b. Affecting multiple sites** | the disease is *respiratory*, not laryngeal — `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf` lists "**Multiple Juvenile Papillomatosis**" among the benign laryngeal tumours, and `entqb-thr10-546` keys **tracheal involvement with airway compromise** and **pulmonary involvement** as real complications |\n| **d. Best treated by repeated laser excision** | `L2` heads its treatment list with "**Laser**, debrider, mitomycine…" and `entqb-thr10-519` keys "**Tracheotomy should be avoided**" as true — repeated endoscopic clearance is the strategy, which is why option **c** is the only false statement here |\n\n**⚠️ Why tracheostomy is avoided is the subject of the bank’s own next question**, `enthd-thr-631`, where it prints its reason on the answer line. Short version: a stoma gives the papillomas a fresh mucosal frontier and carries them down the tracheobronchial tree.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Juvenile RRP = **HPV 6/11, aggressive, multifocal, recurrent, involutes at puberty, low malignant potential**. Adult RRP = **fewer lesions, less recurrence, HIGHER malignant potential**. `entqb-thr10-523` keys the **cauliflower-like** appearance and rejects HPV 16/18. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-524`, `entqb-thr10-519`, `entqb-thr10-523`, `entqb-thr10-546`, `enthd-thr-631`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Juvenile recurrent respiratory papillomatosis is more recurrent and more multifocal than the adult form but LESS liable to malignant transformation, and it involutes at puberty.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.158 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). RRP slide quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`; "Multiple Juvenile Papillomatosis" from `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf`. **⚠️ NO CACHED DECK COMPARES THE MALIGNANT POTENTIAL OF THE JUVENILE AND ADULT FORMS — the specific missing item is the comparison, not the disease, which `L2` teaches in full. THE COMPARISON IS TAKEN FROM THE COURSE’S OWN SECOND BANK, NOT FROM OUTSIDE KNOWLEDGE: `entqb-thr10-524` prints the mirror question over this identical option block and keys the adult form as the more malignant, and `entqb-thr10-519` keys the juvenile-transformation claim as false.** Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-523`, `entqb-thr10-546`, `entqb-thr10-545`, `enthd-thr-631`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-624', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'Which of the following sentences is **true**',
  options:['Leukoplakia is more dangerous than erythroplakia','Erythroplakia is more dangerous than leucoplakia','Both may turn malignant and need surgical MLS with safety margin','A and C','B and C'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Erythroplakia is the more dangerous of the two, and both are premalignant lesions excised with a margin — so B and C are true and A is false.**\n\n**⚠️ NEITHER WORD APPEARS ANYWHERE IN THE CACHED LECTURES.** `plakia` returns **zero** across all thirty-five deck files — no `leukoplakia`, no `leucoplakia`, no `erythroplakia`, under any spelling. **The specific absence is the two named lesions; laryngeal premalignancy as a subject is otherwise absent from the decks too — `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` moves straight from the benign MAP lesions to "Glottic tumors" with nothing in between.**\n\n**The answer therefore comes from the course’s own second bank, not from outside knowledge, and it prints both halves:**\n\n> `entqb-thr10-514` — *"Which of the following is **NOT** true about **leukoplakia**?"* Options: *It occurs due to smoking · **It is treated by MLS with safety margins** · **It is more dangerous than erythroplakia** · It is precancerous.* **Key: "It is more dangerous than erythroplakia."**\n\nThat single item settles this question outright. It keys *leukoplakia is more dangerous* as **false** — which is option A here, and which makes **B** true — and it prints *treated by MLS with safety margins* as a **true** statement about leukoplakia, which is option **C**. **B and C = E.**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Leukoplakia more dangerous than erythroplakia** | ❌ inverted; keyed false at `entqb-thr10-514` |\n| **b. Erythroplakia more dangerous than leucoplakia** | ✔ |\n| **c. Both may turn malignant and need surgical MLS with safety margin** | ✔ — both are premalignant (`entqb-thr10-518` lists both among premalignant laryngeal lesions and keys **laryngoscleroma** as the exception) and both are excised with a margin |\n| **d. A and C** | ❌ carries the inverted statement |\n| **e. B and C** | ✅ **the key** |\n\n**Why erythroplakia is the worse lesion.** A white patch is **hyperkeratosis** — thickened keratin that may or may not hide dysplasia. A red patch has **lost** its surface layers, so the vascular submucosa shows through; red velvety mucosa carries a far higher rate of severe dysplasia or carcinoma in situ at biopsy. **Neither is a diagnosis: both are clinical descriptions that mandate a biopsy.** *(The histological explanation is **not taken from the course material** — no deck and no other bank prints it.)*\n\n**⚠️ Note the bank’s own spelling inside one question: option A prints "Leukoplakia" and option B "leucoplakia".** Both are transcribed as printed. The same eponym-and-spelling drift runs through the neighbouring items — `entqb-thr10-534` prints *Reineke’s*, `entqb-thr10-561` *Reineck’s* and `entqb-thr10-511` *Reinke’s*.\n\nCross-reference: `entqb-thr10-514`, `entqb-thr10-518`, `entqb-thr10-534`, `enthd-thr-637`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Erythroplakia carries a higher malignant potential than leukoplakia; both are premalignant laryngeal lesions requiring microlaryngeal excision with a safety margin.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.159 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). **⚠️ VERIFIED DECK ABSENCE, STATED SPECIFICALLY: the strings `leukoplakia`, `leucoplakia`, `erythroplakia` and the stem `plakia` return ZERO across all thirty-five cached ENT lecture files; `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` passes from the MAP lesions directly to glottic tumours with no premalignancy slide at all.** The ranking and the MLS-with-safety-margin management are taken from Grade Gain’s printed key at `entqb-thr10-514`, per the standing preference for the other bank over outside knowledge; only the hyperkeratosis-versus-denuded-epithelium histology is tagged inline as outside the course material. **The stem prints no question mark and the two spellings *Leukoplakia*/*leucoplakia* appear inside the same item; both transcribed as printed.** Five options, A–E. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-518`, `entqb-thr10-534`, `enthd-thr-637`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-625', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'A **7-year-old child** with **mild hoarseness of voice** visited the doctor and doctor found **regular mass of vocal cyst in his vocal cord**. The best management is….?',
  options:['MLS','Wait and see until puberty and then MLS','Medical treatment','A and C'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**In a child with a mild, stable vocal fold cyst the larynx is still growing, so the operation waits — and this is the reason the bank prints the SAME stem for a 25-year-old at Q626 and keys MLS there instead.** The age is the entire question; see `enthd-thr-626`.\n\n**What `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` gives about the lesion:**\n\n> **Vocal fold cysts** — "collections of **fluid in sac-like formations** on the vocal folds · **Unilateral or bilateral smooth swelling on the membrenous vocal fold, the covering mucosa is NORMAL** · **Females** are more likely than males… the **menstrual cycle may alter the size** of the cyst · two types: **mucus retention** and **epidermoid**."\n>\n> **Treatment:** "**vocal training and speech therapy along with medical interventions to decrease irritation** of the cyst. **In some cases, the cyst grows larger NECESSITATING SURGERY to remove the cyst.**"\n\nSo the deck’s own sequence is **conservative first, surgery when the lesion forces it** — and this cyst is described as *mild* and *regular*, i.e. not forcing anything.\n\n**⚠️ THE DEFERRAL-UNTIL-PUBERTY RULE IS NOT ON ANY CACHED SLIDE.** The specific missing item is **the paediatric timing of microlaryngeal surgery**, not the cyst itself, which `L2` covers in full. The reasoning behind it *(**not taken from the course material**)*: at puberty testosterone lengthens the male vocal fold and the whole larynx enlarges, so a lesion operated at seven is operated on an organ that has not finished growing; microsurgery on a child’s fold risks **permanent scarring of the lamina propria**, and a scarred cover cannot be repaired, whereas a cyst can be re-assessed. Small children also cannot comply with post-operative voice rest — `L2` requires "**2 days of complete voice rest**" even after polyp surgery — nor with the voice therapy that would otherwise be tried first.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **a. MLS** | correct for the adult version of this stem (`enthd-thr-626`), premature here |\n| **c. Medical treatment** | `L2` does use "medical interventions to decrease irritation" — antireflux measures and voice hygiene — but as an **adjunct**, not as the definitive answer; the cyst is a sac of fluid inside the fold and no drug drains it (`entqb-thr9-466` keys that *"treated by voice therapy only"* is **false**) |\n| **d. A and C** | combines the premature operation with the incomplete one |\n\n**⚠️ A CROSS-BANK TENSION, RECORDED AND NOT DISPUTED.** Grade Gain’s `entqb-thr10-540` puts a **vocal fold cyst in an INFANT hoarse since birth** and keys **MLS**, not observation. **The two are not the same question**: that child is symptomatic from birth with a congenital epidermoid cyst, while this one has *mild* hoarseness and an incidental regular cyst. **Both keys are held as printed per the 2026-08-11 ruling; the divergence is noted here and belongs to the reader, not to a correction.**\n\nCross-reference: `enthd-thr-626`, `entqb-thr9-466`, `entqb-thr10-540`, `entqb-thr10-507`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A mild vocal fold cyst in a child is watched until puberty and operated afterwards, because the larynx is still growing and microsurgical scarring of the lamina propria is permanent — the same lesion in an adult is taken straight to MLS.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.159 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). Vocal fold cyst description and its treatment sentence quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`, including the printed *membrenous*. **⚠️ VERIFIED ABSENCE, STATED SPECIFICALLY: no cached deck gives a RULE ON THE TIMING OF PAEDIATRIC MICROLARYNGEAL SURGERY — `L2` covers the cyst itself completely but never mentions deferring an operation until puberty, and no other bank prints the rule either. The laryngeal-growth, lamina-propria-scarring and voice-rest-compliance reasoning is therefore tagged inline as not taken from the course material.** **⚠️ CROSS-BANK DIVERGENCE RECORDED, NEITHER KEY DISPUTED: `entqb-thr10-540` keys MLS for a vocal fold cyst in an infant hoarse since birth. Different vignette, different key, both held.** **⚠️ DELIBERATE GRADED PAIR WITH `enthd-thr-626` — identical option block, identical stem apart from the patient’s age, different keys. DO NOT MERGE.** The stem prints *"is….?"* with four dots, and *"regular mass of vocal cyst"* as printed.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-626', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'A **25-year-old male** with **mild hoarseness of voice** visited the doctor and doctor found **regular mass of vocal cyst in his vocal cord**. The best management is….?',
  options:['MLS','Wait and see until puberty and then MLS','Medical treatment','A and C'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Microlaryngeal surgery. The larynx is fully grown, the lesion is a sac of fluid inside the fold, and nothing short of dissecting it out will remove it.**\n\n`L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` describes the lesion and ends its treatment paragraph with exactly this outcome:\n\n> **Vocal fold cysts** — "collections of **fluid in sac-like formations** on the vocal folds · **Unilateral or bilateral smooth swelling on the membrenous vocal fold, the covering mucosa is normal**" · two types, "**mucus retention**" and "**epidermoid**".\n>\n> **Treatment:** "vocal training and speech therapy along with medical interventions to decrease irritation of the cyst. **In some cases, the cyst grows larger NECESSITATING SURGERY to remove the cyst.**"\n\n**⚠️ THE AGE IS THE WHOLE QUESTION.** The bank prints this stem twice over one identical option block — a **7-year-old** at Q625, keyed *wait and see until puberty*, and this **25-year-old**, keyed *MLS*. **`enthd-thr-625` is its deliberate pair and must not be merged with it.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **b. Wait and see until puberty and then MLS** | **the patient is 25 — puberty is a decade behind him.** It is the correct answer only to the paediatric version of this stem, and offering it here is the trap the pair is built on |\n| **c. Medical treatment** | `L2`’s "medical interventions to decrease irritation" are adjuncts — antireflux measures and voice hygiene. **A cyst is a closed sac inside the fold and no drug drains it**; `entqb-thr9-466` keys *"Treated by voice therapy only"* as **false** about vocal fold cysts for the same reason |\n| **d. A and C** | pairs the right answer with an incomplete one; medical treatment supports the surgery, it is not co-definitive |\n\n**⚠️ A CYST AND A NODULE ARE MANAGED IN OPPOSITE ORDERS, AND THE BANK TESTS BOTH.** A **nodule** is a surface callus of voice abuse and it softens with therapy — `entqb-thr10-528` keys **voice therapy** for a lawyer with bilateral nodules, and `L2` allows surgery for a nodule "**only when it become mature**". A **cyst** is a body buried under an intact cover; therapy cannot reach it, and `entqb-thr10-563` likewise keys **MLS** on a laryngoscopic finding in a hoarse teacher. **Callus → talk it down. Sac → cut it out.**\n\n**What the surgery involves, and why it is microlaryngeal rather than simply "excision".** The cover must be raised as a flap, the sac dissected out **whole**, and the mucosa laid back down; rupturing it leaves epithelial lining behind and invites recurrence. `L2` records the related endpoint — sulcus vocalis "may have relationship with **ruptured congenital epidermoid cysts**". *(The operative detail is **not taken from the course material**; `L2` says only that surgery removes the cyst.)*\n\nCross-reference: `enthd-thr-625`, `entqb-thr9-466`, `entqb-thr10-528`, `entqb-thr10-563`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A vocal fold cyst in an adult is excised by microlaryngeal surgery — it is a closed sac inside the fold that voice therapy and medication cannot resolve, unlike a nodule.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.159 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). Cyst description and treatment sentence quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`, including its printed *membrenous*. **⚠️ DELIBERATE GRADED PAIR WITH `enthd-thr-625` — identical four options in identical order, identical stem except the patient (7-year-old child → 25-year-old male), different keys. DO NOT MERGE.** The microsurgical technique is tagged inline as outside the course material. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr9-466`, `entqb-thr10-528`, `entqb-thr10-563`. The stem prints *"is….?"* with four dots, as printed.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-628', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-paedlar',
  stem:'A **2-year-old male child** with **Face birth mark, weak voice**, **biphasic stridor but no HOV**. Indirect laryngoscopy showed **vascular tumor in left posterior quadrant of subglottis**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Congenital vocal cord paralysis','Congenital subglottic stenosis','Congenital subglottic hemangioma','Vocal polyp'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**A vascular subglottic mass in a child who also has a cutaneous haemangioma on the face is a congenital subglottic haemangioma — and the skin lesion is the single most useful clue in the vignette.**\n\n`L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf`, *Laryngeal Hemangioma*:\n\n> "Def.: **Congenital vascular malformation** arising from mesodermal resets · **SG region** in the larynx → **narrowing of the airway** · **50% associated with hemangioma of the head and neck** · **Female : male = 2:1**."\n>\n> **Diagnosis:** "**Inspiratory stridor at 6 months of age** · **Hemangioma in other areas in H & N (50%)** · Endoscopy · Imaging."\n\n**"Face birth mark" is the other half of that fifty per cent.** Examine the skin before the airway: a facial haemangioma in an infant with stridor converts a guess into a diagnosis.\n\n**The phase of the stridor independently gives the level.** `L6`, *Types of Stridor*: "**Inspiratory** in supraglottic & glottic obstruction · **Inspiratory & Expiratory in SUBGLOTTIC and tracheal obstruction** · **Expiratory** in bronchial obstruction." **Biphasic = subglottic**, which is where the laryngoscopy has already put the tumour — and it is also why the child has a **weak voice but no true hoarseness**: the lesion sits **below the cords**, so the vibrating edge itself is untouched. *(The voice-sparing inference from the level is **not taken from the course material**; `L6` gives the level-to-phase rule but does not draw the voice conclusion.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What the decks say | Fails on |\n|---|---|---|\n| **a. Congenital vocal cord paralysis** | `L6`: onset "**Immediately after birth: SG stenosis, Cong. VC paralysis**". `L2`: congenital palsy is "commonly **unilateral on the left side**" with "**weak hoarse cry and cough**, hoarsness of voice, **mild stridor**" | it is a **mobility** diagnosis, not a mass — and indirect laryngoscopy here shows a **tumour**. It also gives HOV, which the stem denies |\n| **b. Congenital subglottic stenosis** | `L6` calls it the "**3rd common** congenital laryngeal anomaly" and "**the most serious**", presenting **immediately after birth** | right level, wrong tissue — a **fibrous or cartilaginous narrowing**, not a vascular tumour |\n| **c. Congenital subglottic hemangioma** | as above | ✅ **the key** |\n| **d. Vocal polyp** | `L2`: "mainly a disease of **males**, most patients are **smokers** … age peak between **30 and 50** years", on the "**anterior third of the free edge of the true vocal cord**" | an **adult glottic** lesion. In a 2-year-old it is the throwaway option, and a glottic lesion would give HOV and **inspiratory**, not biphasic, stridor |\n\n**⚠️ The left posterior lateral subglottis is where these lesions characteristically sit, and it is the OTHER BANK that prints it, not any slide.** `entqb-thr10-520` offers *"It is most commonly at left posterior lateral quadrant of subglottis"* as one of its **true** statements and keys **biopsy** as the false one. **No cached deck states the quadrant** — `L6` gives only "SG region".\n\n**⚠️ AND IT IS NEVER BIOPSIED.** `L6` lists the diagnostic route as endoscopy and imaging only. Cutting a vascular malformation in a 4.5 mm infant airway bleeds into the one lumen that cannot tolerate it — the bank keys that at `entqb-thr10-520` and `entqb-thr5-289`.\n\n**Treatment buys time until it involutes.** `L6`: "1. **Tracheotomy and follow-up for spontaneous regression (2-4 ys.)** 2. **Corticosteroid** (systemic or intralesional) 3. Laser endoscopic surgery 4. Interferon 5. Cryosurgery 6. External irradiation 7. Surgical excision." **⚠️ Propranolol, now first-line, appears on neither `L6` nor `L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf`** — see `entqb-thr10-548`. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-520`, `entqb-thr10-547`, `entqb-thr5-289`, `entqb-thr8-395`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Congenital subglottic haemangioma — a vascular subglottic mass causing biphasic stridor with a spared voice, half of them with a visible head-and-neck haemangioma; diagnosed by endoscopy and imaging, never by biopsy.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.160 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — the item is paediatric-laryngeal by content and is filed under `ent-paedlar`). Haemangioma definition, subglottic site, 50 % head-and-neck association, F:M 2:1, the onset-timing slide and the level-to-phase rule quoted verbatim from `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf`; the competing diagnoses from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` and `L6`. **⚠️ VERIFIED ABSENCE, STATED SPECIFICALLY: no cached deck names the LEFT POSTERIOR LATERAL QUADRANT — `L6` prints only "SG region in the larynx". The quadrant is Grade Gain’s printed option at `entqb-thr10-520`, preferred to outside knowledge; the voice-sparing inference from the subglottic level is tagged inline as outside the course material.** The bank prints a capital F in *"Face birth mark"* and a full stop before the lowercase *"biphasic"*; both as printed. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-547`, `entqb-thr10-548`, `entqb-thr5-289`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-629', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vocal',
  stem:'A **55-year-old patient** complains with **cough and hemoptysis**. He was diagnosed as **bronchogenic carcinoma**. **After 6 months from the diagnosis, he complained of hoarseness of voice.** The suspected **injury** is most commonly in….?',
  options:['Rt vocal cord','Lt vocal cord','Rt RLN','Lt RLN'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The left recurrent laryngeal nerve, and `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` prints the side in brackets beside the cause:**\n\n> **Unilateral Vocal Fold Paralysis — Malignant:** "**1ST Bronchogenic carcinoma (Lt RLN)** · 2ND Thyroid cancer · 3RD Esophageal · 4TH Skull base (eg. Glomus)."\n\n`L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf` gives the anatomy behind it and makes the restriction explicit:\n\n> **Etiology of VCP — Thoracic causes (LEFT SIDE ONLY):** "**Bronchogenic carcinoma** / Mediastinal lymph node / Esophageal carcinoma · Apical fibrosis / TB · Iatrogenic: Cardiac, esophageal or lung surgery · **Dilated left atrium (Ortner’s syndrome)**."\n>\n> **Incidence:** "**Left VCP is more common (Longer course of left RLN).**"\n\n**Only the left recurrent laryngeal nerve enters the chest.** It loops under the **aortic arch** and climbs back up through the mediastinum, so a hilar or mediastinal tumour can reach it; the right nerve turns at the **subclavian artery**, at the root of the neck, and never descends far enough to meet a bronchogenic carcinoma. *(The two loop points are **not taken from the course material** — `L11` states the left course is longer without naming the vessels.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE TRAP IS THAT TWO OPTIONS NAME THE CORD AND TWO NAME THE NERVE, AND THE STEM ASKS WHERE THE *INJURY* IS.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Rt vocal cord** | wrong side and wrong structure |\n| **b. Lt vocal cord** | **the correct side, and the plausible wrong answer.** The left cord is what becomes **paralysed**, but it is not what is **injured** — it is denervated. The tumour never touches the larynx |\n| **c. Rt RLN** | out of reach of a thoracic tumour; the right nerve is the one at risk **in the neck**, which is why `entqb-thr10-535` keys it for thyroidectomy |\n| **d. Lt RLN** | ✅ **the key** |\n\n**⚠️ A HOARSE VOICE IN A KNOWN LUNG CANCER IS A STAGING EVENT, NOT A VOICE COMPLAINT.** It means mediastinal involvement, and it usually arrives with the disease rather than before it. `L11` files vocal cord paralysis as "**a Sign of another disease**" and builds its whole diagnostic slide around finding the cause — history, neck examination, endoscopy with biopsy, **X-ray chest and CT of brain, skull base, neck and chest**. **In an unexplained unilateral palsy the chest is imaged as a matter of course**, precisely because of this vignette in reverse.\n\n**What the palsy itself will look like.** `L11`: RLN injury paralyses "all intrinsic muscles **EXCEPT cricothyroid**" and leaves the cord "fixed in the **paramedian** position"; unilateral adductor paralysis gives "**Dysphonia · Aspiration**". Management is `L11`’s ladder — six months’ follow-up for compensation, voice therapy, injection augmentation, then **Type I thyroplasty**.\n\nCross-reference: `entqb-thr10-555` (why left palsy predominates), `entqb-thr10-535` (why the right nerve is the surgical one), `enthd-thr-641`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bronchogenic carcinoma is the commonest malignant cause of unilateral vocal fold palsy and it injures the LEFT RLN, the only one that loops into the chest; the paralysed left cord is the consequence, not the site of injury.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.160 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — vocal cord paralysis by content, filed under `ent-vocal`). "1ST Bronchogenic carcinoma (Lt RLN)" quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`; the thoracic-causes list, the "left side only" restriction, the longer-course statement, the paramedian position and the management ladder from `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf`. **The aortic-arch and subclavian loop points are tagged inline as not taken from the course material — `L11` states only that the left course is longer.** The stem prints *"in….?"* with four dots. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-555`, `entqb-thr10-516`, `entqb-thr10-535`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-631', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'All of the following are among **lines of management of recurrent respiratory papillomatosis** **except**?',
  options:['Repeated excision with CO2 LASER','Interferon','Tracheostomy','Mitomycin'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Tracheostomy — and this is one of the very few answer lines in this bank that prints its own reason. Verbatim, as the key is set:**\n\n> `Answer: C (to avoid auto-implantation and spread of infection)`\n\n**The slide says the same thing in two words.** `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`, *Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis*:\n\n> "**HPV 6, 11** · **Most common benign tumor of larynx** · Infant, children · **Spontaneous involution at puberty** · **AVOID TRACHEOSTOMY** · **Laser**, debrider, **mitomycine**, valacyclovir, indol-3-carbinol, isotrentoic acid."\n\nGrade Gain keys the same instruction from the other side: `entqb-thr10-519` prints "**Tracheotomy should be avoided**" as one of its **true** statements.\n\n**What the printed parenthesis means in practice.** Papillomas are **HPV-infected epithelium**, and the virus spreads to any raw mucosal surface it is carried to. A tracheostomy does two things the disease is waiting for: it **cuts a fresh wound through the airway wall**, and it **converts a purely laryngeal disease into a tracheal one** by seeding the stoma and the trachea below it. **Distal spread is the single worst prognostic event in this disease**, and `entqb-thr10-546` keys **tracheal involvement leading to airway compromise** and **pulmonary involvement** as genuine complications. *(The mechanism of implantation at a fresh wound is the reasoning behind the bank’s own phrase and is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Why the other three are lines of management:**\n\n| Option | Grounding |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Repeated excision with CO2 LASER** | `L2` heads its list with "**Laser**"; the disease recurs, so clearance is repeated. `entqb-thr10-545` keys **surgical excision** as first-line |\n| **b. Interferon** | ⚠️ **not on `L2`’s RRP list** — the deck names laser, debrider, mitomycine, valacyclovir, indol-3-carbinol and isotretinoin. **Interferon-alpha is a long-established adjuvant for aggressive, frequently recurring papillomatosis**, reserved for children needing very frequent surgery *(**not taken from the course material** — and note that the one place `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf` does print "Interferon" is in its **haemangioma** treatment list, a different disease; the hit must not be mistaken for grounding here)* |\n| **d. Mitomycin** | `L2` prints "**mitomycine**" in the RRP list verbatim — an antiproliferative applied topically to the raw surface after excision to slow regrowth and reduce scarring |\n\n**⚠️ THE POINT OF THE WHOLE TREATMENT STRATEGY IS `L2`’S OTHER CLAUSE: "spontaneous involution at puberty".** The aim is not cure but **keeping the airway open, with as little trauma to it as possible, until the disease burns out**. Every listed treatment is a way of removing bulk without creating scar — and a tracheostomy is the one intervention that does permanent harm to that plan. **⚠️ It is not absolutely forbidden**: a child obstructing acutely still needs an airway. It is avoided wherever it can be, and decannulated as early as possible. *(The emergency caveat is not on the slide.)*\n\nCross-reference: `enthd-thr-621`, `entqb-thr10-519`, `entqb-thr10-545`, `entqb-thr10-546`, `entqb-thr10-523`.\n\nThe bank prints its own one-line reason for this key, quoted above; the rest of this explanation was written for this bank.',
  objective:'Tracheostomy is avoided in recurrent respiratory papillomatosis because it auto-implants the virus and spreads the disease down the trachea; laser excision, mitomycin and interferon are the accepted lines.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.160 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). **⚠️ THE ANSWER LINE CARRIES A PRINTED EDITORIAL PARENTHESIS — `Answer: C (to avoid auto-implantation and spread of infection)` — quoted VERBATIM and unmarked in the explanation; this entry therefore closes with the alternative paragraph, NOT with the standard "prints no explanation" marker.** RRP slide quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`. **⚠️ VERIFIED ABSENCE, STATED SPECIFICALLY: INTERFERON IS NOT ON `L2`’s RRP TREATMENT LIST — the only `Interferon` string in the cached decks is in the LARYNGEAL HAEMANGIOMA list of `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf`, a different disease, and that hit was opened rather than counted. Its use in RRP is tagged inline as not taken from the course material, as are the implantation mechanism and the emergency-airway caveat.** Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-519` (keys "Tracheotomy should be avoided" as true), `entqb-thr10-545`, `entqb-thr10-546`, `enthd-thr-621`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-636', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'A **50-year-old male who is chronic smoker** developed **hoarseness of voice which was progressive for month**. **Indirect laryngoscope showed SCC.** What is the best management?',
  options:['Total lumpectomy + Chemotherapy','LASER + Radiotherapy','Lumpectomy alone','Radiotherapy alone'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**⚠️ THIS QUESTION IS ONE HALF OF A DELIBERATE PAIR, AND THE MISSING CLAUSE IS THE ANSWER.** Two pages earlier the bank prints the **same 50-year-old chronic smoker with SCC on indirect laryngoscopy and the identical four options in the identical order**, adding four words — *"**but with mobile cords**"* — and keys **B, LASER + Radiotherapy**. That item is `entqb-thr10-553` in this corpus. **Here the mobile-cords clause is absent, so the bank reads the tumour as advanced and keys radical treatment.**\n\n**Cord mobility is the staging word in laryngeal cancer.** A cord that still moves means the tumour has not invaded the vocalis or fixed the cricoarytenoid joint — early disease, curable by a **single organ-preserving modality**. A cord that does not move means deep invasion, and organ preservation is no longer on the table. **`entqb-thr10-553` and this question are the same stem on opposite sides of that line and must never be merged.**\n\n**What the deck gives.** `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`, *Glottic tumors*:\n\n> "Most commonly present with **hoarsness = early present in early stage** · Mostly **squamous cell carcinoma** · Arises from **true vocal cord**, anterior and posterior commissures or vocal processes of arytenoid cartilage · Causes hoarseness early when tumor is small, **leading to early detection and high cure rate** · High cure rate also due to **lack of lymphatics in true vocal cord** · **Treatment: radiotherapy, surgical excision ACCORDING TO THE STAGE**."\n\n**"According to the stage" is the whole of the deck’s treatment teaching**, and it is exactly what the pair is testing.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the key here |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Total lumpectomy + Chemotherapy** | ✅ **the key** — radical excision with systemic treatment, the answer the bank gives when no early-stage clause is printed |\n| **b. LASER + Radiotherapy** | the key to the **mobile-cords** version, `entqb-thr10-553`. Endoscopic laser excision or radiotherapy is the standard for early glottic carcinoma |\n| **c. Lumpectomy alone** | excision with no adjuvant is the management of a **benign** lesion; an invasive carcinoma is not treated by removal alone |\n| **d. Radiotherapy alone** | a legitimate **single-modality** treatment for **early** disease, which is what this stem no longer describes |\n\n**⚠️ TWO THINGS MUST BE SAID PLAINLY, AND NEITHER MOVES THE KEY.**\n\n1. **"Lumpectomy" is a breast word and is not laryngeal vocabulary.** No cached lecture uses it. The bank uses it for cordectomy or laryngectomy; *"Total lumpectomy"* is read as **total laryngectomy**. Transcribed as printed.\n2. **The stem itself carries no stage.** It states only *progressive hoarseness for a month* and *SCC*, and the key rests on the **contrast** with the sibling question rather than on anything printed in this stem. **The key is held exactly as printed per the 2026-08-11 ruling; this is recorded as a property of the pair, not a dispute.** Clinically, chemotherapy has no role in early node-negative glottic disease — but nothing here establishes early disease. *(The staging significance of cord mobility and the role of chemotherapy are **not taken from the course material**; `L2` gives only "according to the stage".)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** After the lesion is seen, the next thing looked for is **cord mobility** — it is the difference between a laser and a laryngectomy. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-553`, `entqb-thr10-562`, `entqb-thr10-521`, `entep-throat-112`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Laryngeal SCC without a documented mobile cord is treated radically — total laryngectomy plus chemotherapy — whereas the same stem WITH mobile cords is early disease treated by laser or radiotherapy alone.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.161 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). Glottic-tumour panel quoted verbatim from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`. **⚠️ SURVIVING HALF OF A DELIBERATE PAIR: the bank prints the SAME stem WITH the clause "but with mobile cords" at its Q627 (book p.159–160), keying LASER + Radiotherapy; that item folded into `entqb-thr10-553` and is not in this batch. THE MOBILE-CORDS CLAUSE IS THE DISCRIMINATOR — DO NOT MERGE THE TWO.** **⚠️ KEY PROPERTY RECORDED, NOT DISPUTED: this stem prints no stage, so the key rests on the contrast with the sibling rather than on the stem itself; held as printed per the 2026-08-11 ruling.** **⚠️ "Total lumpectomy" is the bank’s own word and appears in no cached lecture; read as total laryngectomy, transcribed as printed. The bank also prints "progressive for month" without the article.** The staging role of cord mobility and the role of chemotherapy are tagged inline as not taken from the course material. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-562`, `entqb-thr10-521`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-637', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'Which of the following is a **precancerous laryngeal lesion**?',
  options:['Erythroplakia','Leukoplakia','Adult papilloma','TB larynx','All of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**All four are treated as premalignant by this course’s banks, so the key is *All of the above*.**\n\n**⚠️ VERIFIED ABSENCE, AND IT IS THE CONCEPT RATHER THAN THE DISEASES.** The strings `precancer`, `premalignant` and `plakia` return **zero** across all thirty-five cached ENT lecture files. `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` teaches **TB larynx** and **adult papillomatosis** in full — but as **infections and benign tumours**, never as precursor lesions; it moves from the benign MAP lesions straight to "Glottic tumors" with no premalignancy slide in between. **The two *plakia* lesions are absent altogether.** So the classification itself is what is missing, not the entities.\n\n**The course’s own second bank supplies it, and does so on a five-option list that resolves the whole set:**\n\n> `entqb-thr10-518` — *"Which of the following is **NOT** a premalignant lesion in the larynx?"* Options: **Leukoplakia · Erythroplakia · Adult papilloma · Laryngoscleroma · TB of larynx.** **Key: laryngoscleroma.**\n\n**That single item declares the other four premalignant — and the other four are exactly this question’s options a–d.** Two further items corroborate from other angles: `entqb-thr10-534` and `entep-throat-82` both ask the *except* version and key the entry that is **not** a laryngeal precursor (Reinke’s oedema in one, Plummer-Vinson’s syndrome in the other), leaving leucoplakia, adult papilloma and TB of larynx standing as premalignant in both.\n\n| Option | Why the banks count it |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Erythroplakia** | red velvety mucosa; the more dangerous of the two *plakias* — `entqb-thr10-514` keys *"leukoplakia is more dangerous than erythroplakia"* as **false** (`enthd-thr-624`) |\n| **b. Leukoplakia** | white keratotic patch; `entqb-thr10-514` prints *"It is precancerous"* and *"treated by MLS with safety margins"* among its **true** statements |\n| **c. Adult papilloma** | `L2` gives "**spontaneous involution at puberty**" for the **juvenile** form only, so the adult form is the persisting one — and it is the adult form that transforms (`entqb-thr10-524`, `enthd-thr-621`) |\n| **d. TB larynx** | `L2`: "**2ry to pulmonary** · Now increase as with HIV · **Posterior larynx** · Hoarsness, odynophagia, cough, wt loss, night sweats, heamoptysis". Chronic granulomatous ulceration of the laryngeal mucosa is the reason both banks file it here |\n| **e. All of the above** | ✅ **the key** |\n\n**⚠️ NOTE THE BANK’S OWN SPELLING DRIFT INSIDE THREE PAGES.** This question prints **"Leukoplakia"** and **"TB larynx"**; its neighbours Q622 and Q623 print **"Leucoplakia"** and **"TB of larynx"**. Grade Gain prints the same eponym as *Reineke’s*, *Reineck’s* and *Reinke’s* across one topic. **Quote as printed; do not normalise.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** None of these is a diagnosis — each is an appearance that mandates a **biopsy**, and `entqb-thr10-562` keys exactly that as the next step for a smoker with a laryngeal mass on CT and endoscopy. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-518`, `entqb-thr10-514`, `entqb-thr10-534`, `entep-throat-82`, `enthd-thr-624`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Erythroplakia, leukoplakia, adult papilloma and laryngeal TB are all counted as premalignant laryngeal lesions by this course’s banks; laryngoscleroma is the one that is not.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.162 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). **⚠️ VERIFIED ABSENCE, STATED SPECIFICALLY: the strings `precancer`, `premalignant` and `plakia` return ZERO across all thirty-five cached ENT lecture files. `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` teaches TB larynx and adult papillomatosis fully but NEVER as precursor lesions, and prints no premalignancy slide at all. THE CLASSIFICATION IS TAKEN FROM THE COURSE’S OWN SECOND BANK — `entqb-thr10-518`, whose five-option list keys LARYNGOSCLEROMA as the sole non-premalignant entry and thereby declares this question’s four options premalignant — corroborated by `entqb-thr10-534` and `entep-throat-82`. NOT outside knowledge.** TB-larynx clinical picture quoted verbatim from `L2`. **⚠️ The bank prints "Leukoplakia" and "TB larynx" here where its own Q622/Q623 print "Leucoplakia" and "TB of larynx"; transcribed as printed, not normalised.** Five options, A–E. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-514`, `entqb-thr10-524`, `entqb-thr10-562`, `enthd-thr-624`, `enthd-thr-621`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-639', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vocal',
  stem:'**Bilateral complete vocal cord paralysis (RLN injury)** causes which of the following?',
  options:['Aphonia (or HOV) with aspiration','Stridor','HOV','Exertional dyspnea'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Both cords sit apart and neither can be brought to the midline, so there is no voice and no protection: aphonia with aspiration. The bank prints its own reason on the answer line, verbatim:**\n\n> `Answer: A (Because both VCs are not in touch as they are in cadaveric position)`\n\n**⚠️ THIS IS THE ANCHOR ENTRY OF A FOUR-QUESTION QUARTET.** The bank prints **one identical option block** — *Aphonia (or HOV) with aspiration · Stridor · HOV · Exertional dyspnea* — under the **four permutations of side and completeness**, with **four different keys**. The full grid lives here; the siblings point back at it.\n\n| Question | Lesion | Key |\n|---|---|---|\n| **this one, Q639** | **Bilateral complete** | **A — aphonia (or HOV) with aspiration** |\n| `enthd-thr-640` | **Bilateral partial (abductor)** | **B — stridor** |\n| `enthd-thr-641` | **Unilateral complete** | **C — HOV** |\n| `enthd-thr-642` | **Unilateral partial** | **D — exertional dyspnea** |\n\n**Never fold or merge these four.** Together they are the single best teaching unit in this section.\n\n**The deck’s own grid, which is the grounding for the whole quartet.** `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf`, *Clinical Presentation*:\n\n> | | **Bilateral** | **Unilateral** |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | **Abductor Paralysis** | **Stridor · Normal voice** | Pass unnoticed · Mild aspiration |\n> | **Adductor paralysis** | **Aspiration · Aphonia** | **Dysphonia · Aspiration** |\n\n**Read the bank’s "complete" as the deck’s "adductor" and the bank’s "partial (abductor)" as the deck’s "abductor", and three of the four keys match the slide exactly** — bilateral adductor gives *aspiration and aphonia*, which is this key; bilateral abductor gives *stridor*; unilateral adductor gives *dysphonia*.\n\n**The position of the cords is what generates the symptoms.** `L11`: "**Abductor paralysis: Vocal cord fixed NEAR THE MIDLINE** · **Adductor paralysis: Vocal cord fixed AWAY FROM THE MIDLINE**." Cords stuck **apart** cannot meet to phonate (**aphonia**) and cannot close to protect the airway (**aspiration**), but they leave the airway wide, so there is **no stridor**. That is precisely the state the bank’s parenthesis describes as *not in touch*.\n\n**Why the other three options belong to the siblings:** *Stridor* requires both cords **near the midline** — the bilateral abductor picture (`enthd-thr-640`); *HOV* is one cord failing while the other compensates (`enthd-thr-641`); *exertional dyspnea* is a partial unilateral narrowing that only shows under load (`enthd-thr-642`).\n\n**⚠️ ONE CONFLICT BETWEEN THE PRINTED PARENTHESIS AND THE COURSE’S OWN SLIDE, RECORDED AND NOT CORRECTED.** The stem says **RLN injury**; the parenthesis says the cords are in the **cadaveric position**. `L11` assigns those to different lesions: "Injury of the **RLN**: Paralysis of all intrinsic muscles EXCEPT Cricothyroid ms. **Paralyzed VC fixed in the PARAMEDIAN position.** Injury of the **Vagus nerve**: Paralysis of ALL intrinsic muscles. **Paralyzed VC fixed in the CADAVERIC position.**" **By the deck, a pure RLN injury does not give a cadaveric cord.** The bank’s key — aphonia with aspiration — is nevertheless the deck’s own entry for bilateral adductor palsy, so **the key stands and only the printed reason diverges.** Both are recorded; neither is altered.\n\n**Management, since the airway is not the problem here but the chest is.** `L11`, *Bil. adductor VC paralysis*: "1. **Care of feeding**: Nasogastric tube · Gastrostomy. 2. **Care of the chest.** 3. Surgical intervention: **Laryngeal closure · Tracheal diversion**." Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-640`, `enthd-thr-641`, `enthd-thr-642`, `enthd-thr-629`.\n\nThe bank prints its own one-line reason for this key, quoted above; the rest of this explanation was written for this bank.',
  objective:'Bilateral complete (adductor) vocal cord paralysis leaves both cords away from the midline — aphonia and aspiration with a patent airway — and is managed by protecting feeding and the chest, not the airway.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.162 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — vocal cord paralysis by content, filed under `ent-vocal`). **⚠️ THE ANSWER LINE CARRIES A PRINTED EDITORIAL PARENTHESIS — `Answer: A (Because both VCs are not in touch as they are in cadaveric position)` — quoted VERBATIM and unmarked; this entry closes with the alternative paragraph, NOT the standard "prints no explanation" marker.** Clinical-presentation grid, the abductor/adductor cord positions, the RLN-versus-vagus rule and the bilateral-adductor management quoted verbatim from `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf`. **⚠️⚠️ THE COURSE’S OWN SLIDE CONTRADICTS THE PRINTED RATIONALE, NOT THE KEY: `L11` assigns the CADAVERIC position to VAGUS injury and the PARAMEDIAN position to RLN injury, while the stem says RLN injury and the parenthesis says cadaveric. The key matches `L11`’s own bilateral-adductor row and is held as printed; the divergence is recorded in the explanation per the 2026-08-11 ruling.** **⚠️ ANCHOR OF THE Q639–Q642 QUARTET — one identical option block, four permutations, four different keys. THE SHARED GRID LIVES IN THIS ENTRY; the three siblings point at it. NEVER FOLD OR MERGE ANY OF THE FOUR.** Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-640`, `enthd-thr-641`, `enthd-thr-642`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-640', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vocal',
  stem:'**Bilateral partial (abductor) vocal cord paralysis (RLN injury)** causes which of the following?',
  options:['Aphonia (or HOV) with aspiration','Stridor','HOV','Exertional dyspnea'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Both cords are stuck near the midline, so the voice is preserved and the airway is not: stridor. The bank prints its own reason on the answer line, verbatim:**\n\n> `Answer: B (Because both VCs are directly adherent to each other)`\n\n**`L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf` keys this row the same way and adds the half the options omit:**\n\n> **Clinical Presentation — Abductor Paralysis, Bilateral: "STRIDOR · NORMAL VOICE."**\n>\n> **Incidence:** "**Abductor paralysis: Vocal cord fixed NEAR THE MIDLINE** · Adductor paralysis: Vocal cord fixed away from the midline."\n\n**Two cords held together at the midline still meet, so phonation is normal — and that is exactly what makes this the dangerous one.** The patient sounds well and cannot breathe. **A normal voice is not reassurance in a post-thyroidectomy patient; it is the finding that points at this diagnosis.** `L11` gives the causes: bilateral VCP is "**Iatrogenic 80%** · Malignancy 10% · Neurologic 5% · Intubation · Idiopathic · Endocrine or metabolic". **The bank prints the emergency version at its own Q644** — a 40-year-old with stridor, intercostal and epigastric retraction and mild cyanosis after thyroidectomy — and keys **bilateral abductor vocal cord paralysis**; `entqb-thr10-526` puts the same picture in Grade Gain.\n\n**⚠️ This is one of a QUARTET printed over one identical option block. The shared grid — all four permutations, all four keys, and the deck’s own presentation table — lives in `enthd-thr-639` and is not repeated here.** In short: the other three options belong to bilateral **complete** (aphonia with aspiration, `enthd-thr-639`), unilateral **complete** (HOV, `enthd-thr-641`) and unilateral **partial** (exertional dyspnea, `enthd-thr-642`). **Never fold or merge the four.**\n\n**Management is airway first, and `L11` orders it:**\n\n> **Management of Bil. abductor VC paralysis:** "1. **Care of the airway**: Endotracheal intubation · **Tracheostomy**. 2. **Follow-up for 6 months** for spontaneous recovery. 3. **Lateralization procedures**: External arytenoidectomy · **Endoscopic arytenoidectomy / posterior cordotomy**. 4. **Laryngeal re-innervation**."\n\n**⚠️ Note the order of the trade-off**: every lateralisation procedure opens the airway **at the cost of the voice** — the cords are moved apart, so they meet less well. That is why six months of follow-up comes before any of them, and why `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf` teaches the posterior cordotomy: cutting **posteriorly** enlarges the breathing part of the glottis while sparing the anterior part, which is what phonates. *(The voice-versus-airway trade-off is not spelled out on the slides.)*\n\nCross-reference: `enthd-thr-639` (shared grid), `enthd-thr-641`, `enthd-thr-642`, `entqb-thr10-526`.\n\nThe bank prints its own one-line reason for this key, quoted above; the rest of this explanation was written for this bank.',
  objective:'Bilateral abductor (partial) palsy fixes both cords near the midline — stridor with a NORMAL voice — and it is managed by securing the airway first, then lateralisation at the cost of voice.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.162 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — filed under `ent-vocal` by content). **⚠️ THE ANSWER LINE CARRIES A PRINTED EDITORIAL PARENTHESIS — `Answer: B (Because both VCs are directly adherent to each other)` — quoted VERBATIM and unmarked; this entry closes with the alternative paragraph, NOT the standard "prints no explanation" marker.** Presentation row, cord positions, bilateral aetiology percentages and the four-step management quoted verbatim from `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf`. **⚠️ MEMBER OF THE Q639–Q642 QUARTET — one identical option block, four permutations, four keys. The shared grid lives in `enthd-thr-639`; this entry points at it rather than repeating it. NEVER FOLD OR MERGE.** The voice-versus-airway trade-off is tagged inline as outside the course material. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-526`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-641', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vocal',
  stem:'**Unilateral complete vocal cord paralysis (RLN injury)** causes which of the following?',
  options:['Aphonia (or HOV) with aspiration','Stridor','HOV','Exertional dyspnea'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**One cord is out of action and the other still works, so the voice is hoarse rather than absent, and the airway is untouched. The bank prints its own reason on the answer line, verbatim:**\n\n> `Answer: C (Because the paralysed VC is kept in cadaveric position)`\n\n**`L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf` gives the same row:**\n\n> **Clinical Presentation — Adductor paralysis, Unilateral: "DYSPHONIA · Aspiration."**\n\nDysphonia is the deck’s word for what the bank prints as **HOV**. The mechanism is a **glottic gap**: the paralysed cord cannot come to the midline, so the healthy cord meets nothing and air escapes across an incompletely closed glottis — a breathy, weak, easily fatigued voice. `L11` also confirms the epidemiology of this half of the quartet: "**Unilateral VCP > Bilateral VCP** · **Bilateral VCP is more symptomatic**."\n\n**⚠️ QUARTET MEMBER — the shared grid, the four keys and the deck’s full presentation table are in `enthd-thr-639` and are not repeated here.** The three rejected options belong to the siblings: *aphonia with aspiration* to bilateral complete (`enthd-thr-639`), *stridor* to bilateral abductor (`enthd-thr-640`), *exertional dyspnea* to unilateral partial (`enthd-thr-642`). **Never fold or merge the four.** **⚠️ And the same conflict between the printed parenthesis and `L11`’s RLN-versus-vagus rule applies here — RLN injury gives a PARAMEDIAN cord on the slide, not a cadaveric one; it is recorded in full in `enthd-thr-639` and neither key is altered.**\n\n**Why aspiration is on the deck’s row but not on the bank’s option here.** `L11` lists *aspiration* alongside dysphonia for the unilateral adductor case; the bank has bundled aspiration into option **a** and attached it to the bilateral form, so on this option list **HOV alone is the answer that fits**. Clinically, unilateral palsy does cause some aspiration to thin liquids until the healthy cord compensates across the midline.\n\n**Management — the one part of this topic with a real ladder.** `L11`, *Management of unilateral VC paralysis*:\n\n> "1. **Follow-up for 6 months** for spontaneous recovery / **Compensation**. 2. **Voice therapy.** 3. **Injection augmentation (Voice gap 1-3 mm)** — Temporary: autologous e.g. **fat, collagen, fascia**, or heterogeneous e.g. **hyaluronic acid**; Permanent / Long lasting: **Hydroxyapatite (Radiesse voice)**. 4. **Framework surgery — Medialization Laryngoplasty = Type 1 Thyroplasty**."\n\n`L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` describes the same operation from the other side: "**Type 1: to medialize the paralysed cord to phonate well and close the gap**". **Everything on that ladder is aimed at closing the gap — either by bulking the paralysed cord out or by pushing it inwards.** Note the gap size in `L11`’s own parenthesis: injection is for a **1–3 mm** gap; anything larger needs the framework operation. **And note the six months: `L11` requires laryngeal EMG "1-6 months after the onset" to separate paresis from paralysis, and the whole first step is waiting to see whether the nerve regenerates or the opposite cord compensates.**\n\nCross-reference: `enthd-thr-639` (shared grid), `enthd-thr-640`, `enthd-thr-642`, `enthd-thr-629`, `entqb-thr10-535`.\n\nThe bank prints its own one-line reason for this key, quoted above; the rest of this explanation was written for this bank.',
  objective:'Unilateral complete (adductor) palsy leaves a glottic gap — hoarseness with some aspiration and no airway compromise — managed by six months’ observation, voice therapy, injection augmentation for a 1–3 mm gap, then type 1 thyroplasty.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.162 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — filed under `ent-vocal` by content). **⚠️ THE ANSWER LINE CARRIES A PRINTED EDITORIAL PARENTHESIS — `Answer: C (Because the paralysed VC is kept in cadaveric position)` — quoted VERBATIM and unmarked; this entry closes with the alternative paragraph, NOT the standard "prints no explanation" marker.** Presentation row, incidence statements, the EMG timing and the four-step management ladder quoted verbatim from `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf`; the type 1 thyroplasty description from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`. **⚠️ THE SAME SLIDE-VERSUS-PARENTHESIS CONFLICT AS `enthd-thr-639`: `L11` gives the CADAVERIC position to VAGUS injury and the PARAMEDIAN position to RLN injury. Recorded there in full; key held as printed.** **⚠️ MEMBER OF THE Q639–Q642 QUARTET; the shared grid lives in `enthd-thr-639`. NEVER FOLD OR MERGE.** The glottic-gap mechanism and the thin-liquid aspiration point are not spelled out on the slides. Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-640`, `enthd-thr-642`, `enthd-thr-629`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-642', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vocal',
  stem:'**Unilateral partial vocal cord paralysis (RLN injury)** causes which of the following?',
  options:['Aphonia (or HOV) with aspiration','Stridor','HOV','Exertional dyspnea'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**One cord fixed near the midline narrows the glottis a little — not enough to be heard at rest, but enough to limit airflow when the patient needs it. The bank prints its own reason on the answer line, verbatim, and this one opens with a lower-case letter:**\n\n> `Answer: D (because the paralysed VC is adducted so it interferes with inhalation causing dyspnea that is most prominent upon exertion)`\n\n**The mechanics are `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf`’s own: "Abductor paralysis: Vocal cord fixed NEAR THE MIDLINE."** A partial (abductor) lesion on one side leaves that cord sitting close to the midline while the other still abducts, so the glottis opens to perhaps half its normal aperture. At rest that is ample; on exertion, when minute ventilation rises several-fold, the fixed narrowing becomes the limiting step. **The bank’s parenthesis names inspiration specifically, and that is right — the glottis is an extrathoracic airway, so negative inspiratory pressure pulls the narrowed segment further shut, while expiration blows it open.** *(The extrathoracic-airway mechanism is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**⚠️⚠️ THE COURSE’S OWN SLIDE DOES NOT PRINT THIS SYMPTOM, AND IT IS THE ONE PLACE IN THE QUARTET WHERE THE DECK AND THE BANK DIVERGE ON THE ANSWER RATHER THAN ON THE REASONING.** `L11`’s presentation grid gives, for **Abductor Paralysis · Unilateral**: "**Pass unnoticed · Mild aspiration**". **The deck says this lesion is usually silent; the bank says it causes exertional dyspnoea.** The two are reconcilable — a lesion that "passes unnoticed" in ordinary life may still declare itself under load, and `L11` is describing the resting patient — but **the slide as printed does not support the key, and that is recorded rather than smoothed over. The key is held exactly as printed, per the 2026-08-11 ruling; no key is ever disputed here.** ⚠️ This is a *course-contradicts-key* item, not an objection on clinical grounds: the divergence is between the bank and `L11`’s own table, quoted above.\n\n**⚠️ QUARTET MEMBER — the shared grid and the other three keys live in `enthd-thr-639` and are not repeated.** The rejected options: *aphonia with aspiration* is bilateral complete (`enthd-thr-639`); *stridor* needs **both** cords near the midline (`enthd-thr-640`); *HOV* is unilateral **complete**, where the paralysed cord sits away from the midline and leaves a glottic gap (`enthd-thr-641`). **Never fold or merge the four.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The four permutations reduce to two questions: **how many cords, and where do they end up?** Cords **apart** cost the **voice** and the airway’s protection; cords **near the midline** cost the **airway** and spare the voice. Bilateral doubles whichever cost applies. Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-639` (shared grid), `enthd-thr-640`, `enthd-thr-641`.\n\nThe bank prints its own one-line reason for this key, quoted above; the rest of this explanation was written for this bank.',
  objective:'Unilateral partial (abductor) palsy fixes one cord near the midline, narrowing the glottis enough to cause dyspnoea on exertion while leaving voice and resting breathing intact.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.163 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — filed under `ent-vocal` by content). **⚠️ THE ANSWER LINE CARRIES A PRINTED EDITORIAL PARENTHESIS — `Answer: D (because the paralysed VC is adducted so it interferes with inhalation causing dyspnea that is most prominent upon exertion)` — quoted VERBATIM, including the LOWER-CASE "because" where Q639–Q641 print "Because"; unmarked, and this entry closes with the alternative paragraph, NOT the standard "prints no explanation" marker.** **⚠️⚠️ COURSE-CONTRADICTS-KEY, RECORDED AND NOT DISPUTED: `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf`’s Clinical Presentation grid gives UNILATERAL ABDUCTOR paralysis as "Pass unnoticed · Mild aspiration" — it does not print exertional dyspnoea. The bank’s key is held exactly as printed per the 2026-08-11 ruling and the divergence is quoted in the explanation. This is a slide-versus-key divergence, NOT a clinical objection.** The near-midline cord position is `L11`’s own; the extrathoracic-airway mechanism is tagged inline as outside the course material. **⚠️ MEMBER OF THE Q639–Q642 QUARTET; shared grid in `enthd-thr-639`. NEVER FOLD OR MERGE.**' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-646', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hoarse',
  stem:'**Minor associated pathological lesions** are vocal fold lesions caused by ….?',
  options:['Organic dysphonia','Voice abuse','Hysteria','Microlaryngosurgery'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**MAP lesions are the lesions that vocal abuse builds on an otherwise healthy fold — nodules, polyps, cysts, granulomas, Reinke’s oedema.** They are the middle ground between a voice disorder with **no** lesion and a voice disorder with **disease**.\n\n**`L8) Introduction to phoniatrics.pdf` prints the classification as three parallel columns, and that layout is the answer to this question:**\n\n> **Voice disorders**\n>\n> | **Functional** | **Minimal associated pathological lesions** | **Organic** |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | Hyperfunctional childhood dysphonia · Mutational voice disorders · **Hyperfuctional dysphonia** · Hypofunctional dysphonia · Phonasthenia · Ventricular dysphonia · Habitual aphonia | **vocal fold nodules · vocal fold polyp · Vocal fold cyst · Reinke’s oedema · Contact ganuloma** | |\n\n**The MAP column sits beside "Functional" and beside "Organic", and its own neighbour on the functional side is HYPERFUNCTIONAL dysphonia — i.e. too much force.** That is the link the key names: **voice abuse.**\n\n**`L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` supplies the abuse for each lesion by name:** nodules are "found more in **women, children, professional singers, lecturers**"; contact granuloma follows mucosa "**crushed repetitively** against the opposite side" and is rested when "**forceful speaking is the primary cause**"; Reinke’s oedema lists "**chronic voice abuse**" among its causes; and Grade Gain prints "**Acute screaming usually causes polyps**" as a true statement at `entqb-thr10-505`.\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Organic dysphonia** | **a CATEGORY, not a cause** — and in `L8`’s scheme it is the category *next to* MAP, not the one MAP belongs to. A lesion cannot be caused by the class it is being distinguished from |\n| **c. Hysteria** | the **psychogenic/functional** group, where there is **no lesion at all**. `L2`: "Psychogenic: **Conversion = hysterical**: more in females — **Order her to cough she is ok**." The intact cough is the bedside test, and it is exactly what the bank’s own Q620 turns into a vignette |\n| **d. Microlaryngosurgery** | the **treatment**, not the cause. `L2` lists MLS under *Surgical treatment* |\n\n**⚠️ A DECK-VERSUS-DECK DISCREPANCY IN THE CLASSIFICATION, BOTH RECORDED, NEITHER CORRECTED.** `L8` makes MAP a **third column beside Organic**. `L2` files it **inside** the organic group — "Organic causes: • **Minimally associated pathology (MAP)**: Vc. Nodule · Vc. Polyp · Vc. Granuloma · Vc. Cyst · **Vc. Fibrosis · Vc. Sulcus** · Inflammatory, infective · Neoplastic · Congenital · Vc. Paralysis and fixation · Gastrooesophegeal reflux · Age related dysphonia". **The two lists also differ in membership** — `L2` adds fibrosis and sulcus, `L8` adds Reinke’s oedema and contact granuloma. **Either way option (a) fails**, because a category is not an aetiology.\n\n**⚠️ AND ONE MORE TENSION WORTH HOLDING: `L2`’s reflux slide states "Causes MOST of MAP lesions" of GERD**, and `entqb-thr9-465` prints "**They are predisposed by GERD**" as a **true** common feature of MAP lesions. **Reflux and voice abuse are both aetiologies of this group; only voice abuse is offered on this option list.**\n\n**⚠️ Note the name.** The bank writes **"Minor** associated pathological lesions"; `L8` writes **"Minimal** associated pathological lesions"; `L2` writes **"Minimally** associated pathology (MAP)". **Three names for one group; quoted as each source prints it.** The abbreviation is used unexplained by this bank at its own Q619, five pages earlier, and defined only here.\n\nCross-reference: `entqb-thr10-561`, `entqb-thr9-491`, `entqb-thr9-465`, `entqb-thr10-505`, `enthd-thr-617`, `enthd-thr-618`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Minimal associated pathological (MAP) lesions — nodules, polyps, cysts, contact granuloma, Reinke’s oedema — are the vocal-abuse lesions of an otherwise healthy fold, distinct from functional (no lesion) and organic (disease) dysphonia.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.163 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice). Three-column voice-disorder classification quoted verbatim from `L8) Introduction to phoniatrics.pdf`, including its printed *Hyperfuctional* and *Contact ganuloma*; the per-lesion abuse histories and the psychogenic cough test from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`. Straddles `ent-phon` — the classification is the phoniatrics deck’s — and is filed under `ent-hoarse` to sit with its siblings `entqb-thr10-561` and `entqb-thr9-491`, which ask the same group from the *except* end. **⚠️ DECK-VERSUS-DECK DISCREPANCY RECORDED, NEITHER CORRECTED: `L8` makes MAP a third category BESIDE organic; `L2` files it INSIDE the organic group, and the two membership lists differ (L2 adds fibrosis and sulcus; L8 adds Reinke’s oedema and contact granuloma).** **⚠️ SECOND TENSION RECORDED: `L2`’s GERD slide states reflux "Causes most of MAP lesions" and `entqb-thr9-465` prints GERD predisposition as a true feature — voice abuse is simply the only aetiology on this option list.** **⚠️ THREE NAMES FOR ONE GROUP — "Minor" (this bank), "Minimal" (`L8`), "Minimally" (`L2`); each quoted as printed.** The bank uses the abbreviation *MAP lesions* unexplained at its own Q619 and defines it only here. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr10-505`, `enthd-thr-617`, `enthd-thr-618`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-647', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vocal',
  stem:'In a case of **superior laryngeal nerve paralysis only**, the patient complains of which of the following?',
  options:['Low pitched voice','Respiratory distress','Throaty muffled voice','Repeated cough and choking'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The superior laryngeal nerve drives the one muscle that TENSES the cord, and a cord that cannot be tensed cannot reach a high note. The bank prints its own reason on the answer line, verbatim:**\n\n> `Answer: A (due to paralysis of cricothyroid that is responsible for high pitched voice)`\n\n**Both decks print the anatomy that makes this work.** `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf`:\n\n> **Nerve supply of the larynx – Vagus nerve. 1. Superior Laryngeal nerve (SLN): ❖External branch – MOTOR TO CRICOTHYROID MUSCLE · ❖Internal branch - SENSORY.** **2. Recurrent Laryngeal nerve (RLN): ❖Motor to ALL muscles of the larynx (EXCEPT Cricothyroid) · ❖Sensory.**\n>\n> **Anatomical considerations:** "**Tensor to the VC: Cricothyroid ms.** (also has adductor effect)."\n\n`L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` says it in three words: "**Action of cricothyroid: Tensor of the cord**."\n\n**Pitch is a function of cord tension.** The cricothyroid tilts the thyroid cartilage forwards on the cricoid, lengthening and stretching the fold; the tighter the fold, the faster it vibrates and the higher the pitch. **Lose it and the upper register goes first** — the voice sits low, tires quickly and loses its range. **This is the palsy that ends a singing career while leaving ordinary speech nearly normal**, and it is the classic sequel of thyroid surgery near the superior pole, where the external branch runs with the superior thyroid vessels. *(The surgical relation to the superior thyroid pedicle is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **b. Respiratory distress** | abduction is the job of the **posterior cricoarytenoid**, which `L2` calls "**The only abductor**" and which `L11` places under the **RLN**. An SLN lesion leaves every abductor and adductor working, so **the airway is untouched** |\n| **c. Throaty muffled voice** | a **muffled or "hot potato" voice** means something above the glottis is bulking out the supraglottis or pharynx — `L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf` lists "**Muffled voice**" among the features of **acute epiglottitis**, alongside fever, severe odynophagia and drooling. It is an obstruction sign, not a nerve sign |\n| **d. Repeated cough and choking** | ⚠️ **the strongest distractor, and the deck is why.** `L11` states the SLN’s **internal branch is SENSORY** — it supplies the supraglottic mucosa, so an SLN lesion does blunt the laryngeal protective reflex, and `L2`’s congenital-palsy slide notes "**Aspiration if the affection proximal to the origin of the superior laryngeal nerve**". **The option is therefore defensible on the course’s own anatomy.** What makes A the better answer on this list is that the bank asks what the patient **complains of** in an SLN palsy **only**: with both cords still adducting normally, glottic closure is intact and unilateral sensory loss is usually compensated, whereas the loss of the tensor is heard in every sentence. *(This weighing is the reading of a strong distractor and no slide contradicts the key; it is recorded as an observation, not as a dispute — the key is held exactly as printed.)* |\n\n**Clinical bottom line. Divide the vagal supply and the symptoms divide with it: RLN = the cord’s POSITION (hoarseness, aspiration, stridor); SLN = the cord’s TENSION (pitch) and the larynx’s SENSATION.** Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-639` (the RLN quartet), `enthd-thr-641`, `entqb-thr10-535`.\n\nThe bank prints its own one-line reason for this key, quoted above; the rest of this explanation was written for this bank.',
  objective:'The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve supplies cricothyroid, the tensor of the cord, so an isolated SLN palsy costs vocal pitch and range — not airway, not glottic closure.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.164 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — the item sits immediately before the "7. Stridor & tracheostomy" heading; filed under `ent-vocal` by content). **⚠️ THE ANSWER LINE CARRIES A PRINTED EDITORIAL PARENTHESIS — `Answer: A (due to paralysis of cricothyroid that is responsible for high pitched voice)` — quoted VERBATIM and unmarked; this entry closes with the alternative paragraph, NOT the standard "prints no explanation" marker.** SLN/RLN supply, the cricothyroid-as-tensor line and the "only abductor" statement quoted verbatim from `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf` and `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`; the muffled-voice attribution from the acute-epiglottitis slide of `L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf`. **⚠️ DISTRACTOR (d) IS PARTLY SUPPORTED BY THE COURSE’S OWN ANATOMY — `L11` prints the internal branch as SENSORY and `L2` notes aspiration when the lesion is proximal to the SLN origin. NO SLIDE CONTRADICTS THE PRINTED KEY; the weighing of the two options is recorded in the explanation as an observation, not as a key dispute.** The pitch mechanism and the surgical relation to the superior thyroid pedicle are tagged inline as outside the course material. Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-641`, `entqb-thr10-535`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-648', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-stridor',
  stem:'**Biphasic stridor** may be due to …..?',
  options:['Bronchial asthma','Subglottic tumor','Vocal cord paralysis','Glottic carcinoma'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The phase of the stridor gives the level of the obstruction, and `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf` prints the rule as a three-line slide:**\n\n> **Types of Stridor** — "**Inspiratory** in **supraglottic & glottic** obstruction · **Inspiratory & Expiratory** in **SUBGLOTTIC and TRACHEAL** obstruction · **Expiratory** in **bronchial** obstruction."\n\n**Biphasic = subglottic or tracheal. Only one option sits at that level.**\n\n**Why the level dictates the phase.** The subglottis and trachea straddle the thoracic inlet, so a narrowing there is squeezed from **both** directions — inspiration pulls the extrathoracic part shut, expiration pushes the intrathoracic part shut. A lesion above the cords is affected only by the inspiratory pull; one in the bronchi only by the expiratory push. *(The pressure argument is the reasoning behind `L6`’s rule and is not printed on the slide.)*\n\n**`L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf` corroborates the rule with a disease.** Croup — acute laryngotracheobronchitis, a **subglottic** inflammation — is listed with "Low grade fever · Barking cough · Hoarseness of voice · **Biphasic stridor**" and the "**Steeple sign**". **Subglottic disease, biphasic noise.**\n\n| Option | Level | Deck grounding | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **a. Bronchial asthma** | lower airway | `L6` explicitly separates it from stridor: "**WHEEZING**: whistling sound, **mainly expiratory**, arising from **lower airway obstruction — asthma**, COD, foreign body in trachea" | ❌ not stridor at all, and expiratory |\n| **b. Subglottic tumor** | **subglottic** | the level `L6` assigns to biphasic stridor; `L6` also files malignant subglottic tumours as causing "**Early stridor**" against the "**Late stridor**" of glottic and supraglottic ones | ✅ **the key** |\n| **c. Vocal cord paralysis** | **glottic** | `L6` puts glottic obstruction in the **inspiratory** line; `L11) Vocal cord paralysis.pdf` gives stridor to **bilateral abductor** palsy, but at the level of the cords | ❌ right symptom, wrong phase |\n| **d. Glottic carcinoma** | **glottic** | same line — inspiratory. And `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf` notes glottic tumours "**present with hoarsness = early present in early stage**", so hoarseness, not stridor, is their opening symptom | ❌ wrong phase, and it declares itself by voice first |\n\n**⚠️ Do not confuse *early/late stridor* with *inspiratory/biphasic*.** `L6` uses both axes on the same lesions: a **subglottic** tumour narrows a small airway from the start, so stridor comes **early** in the disease **and** is **biphasic** by level; a **glottic** tumour is heard as hoarseness long before it obstructs, so its stridor is **late** — and **inspiratory** when it comes.\n\n**Clinical bottom line — the phase is a free localisation, before any instrument is picked up: inspiratory → above or at the cords · biphasic → subglottis or trachea · expiratory → bronchi.** The bank uses the same rule for a subglottic haemangioma at `enthd-thr-628`, and Grade Gain prints biphasic stridor as a true feature of that lesion at `entqb-thr10-520`. Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-628`, `entqb-thr10-520`, `enthd-thr-649`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Stridor phase localises the obstruction — inspiratory above or at the glottis, BIPHASIC in the subglottis and trachea, expiratory in the bronchi; asthma gives an expiratory wheeze, not stridor.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.164 (Throat ch.6, Hoarseness of voice — stridor by content, filed under `ent-stridor`; the "7. Stridor & tracheostomy" heading prints immediately after this question). *Types of Stridor*, the stridor/stertor/wheeze definitions and the early-versus-late tumour rule quoted verbatim from `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf`; the croup features from `L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf`; the glottic-tumour panel from `L2) Hoarsness of Voice.pdf`. The intrathoracic/extrathoracic pressure argument is the reasoning behind the deck’s rule and is tagged inline as outside the course material. The stem prints *"…..?"* with five dots, as printed. Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-628`, `entqb-thr10-520`.' },

{ id:'enthd-thr-649', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-paedlar',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common laryngeal congenital cause of stridor**?',
  options:['Laryngomalacia','Vascular ring','Vocal cord paralysis','Congenital subglottic stenosis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Laryngomalacia, and both paediatric decks state it outright.**\n\n`L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf`:\n\n> **Laryngomalacia – Incidence:** "**Most common congenital laryngeal anomaly (60%)** · **Most common cause of chronic stridor in infants** · More common in **males** · Associated with **GERD in 80 – 90%** of cases · Synchronous airway anomalies in 15 – 20%."\n\n`L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf` says the same thing twice over:\n\n> "**The most common congenital anomaly of the larynx and most frequent cause of stridor in children** · Accounts for **60% of laryngeal problem in infants** · **Boys : Girls = 2:1** · More common in **premature infants**."\n\n**⚠️ THE WORD *LARYNGEAL* IS THE HINGE OF THIS QUESTION, BECAUSE THE BANK PRINTS THE MIRROR OF IT ON THE NEXT LINE.** Its Q650 asks for the most common **EXTRA**laryngeal congenital cause of stridor over **this identical option block** and keys **vascular ring** — Grade Gain prints the same question and the same key at `entqb-thr8-404`. `L6` puts vascular ring in its own list: "**Congenital: Extralaryngeal Diseases** · Cystic hygroma · Congenital goiter · Thymic remnants & Cysts · Mediastinal swellings e.g. teratoma · Tracheal stenosis · Tracheomalacia · **Vascular ring**." **Two questions, one option block, one word apart.**\n\n**Why the other two laryngeal options rank below it — `L12` prints the congenital anomalies in DESCENDING ORDER OF INCIDENCE, and the option list is simply that list re-shuffled:**\n\n> **Congenital Laryngeal Anomalies — Classification, Incidence of occurrence:** "1. **Laryngomalacia** · 2. **Congenital VC paralysis** · 3. **Congenital subglottic stenosis** · 4. Laryngeal web · 5. Posterior Laryngeal cleft."\n\n| Option | Rank | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **b. Vascular ring** | — | **extralaryngeal**; the key to the sibling question |\n| **c. Vocal cord paralysis** | 2nd | laryngeal and a real cause of stridor, but second; `L6` puts its onset "**immediately after birth**" and `entqb-thr5-252` keys laryngomalacia over it on a four-option list of the same kind |\n| **d. Congenital subglottic stenosis** | 3rd | ⚠️ **commonest is not most dangerous** — `L6` calls it "**3rd common** congenital laryngeal anomaly" **and** "**The most serious congenital anomaly**", with a lumen "less than 4 mm in a full-term newborn" against a normal 4.5 mm |\n\n**What laryngomalacia is and how it behaves.** `L12` — "**Laryngo = larynx, malacia = abnormal flaccidity**", a "**Collapse of the supraglottic components during inspiration**: Epiglottis · Aryepiglottic folds · Arytenoids". The stridor is therefore **inspiratory only** and **high-pitched**; it "starts **few weeks after birth** → increase in severity over **6-9 months**", is "**increased on supine, feeding & agitation**", "**decreased on neck extension & prone position**", and "**resolves spontaneously 18 – 24 months in 80 - 90% of cases**", with **normal voice, normal feeding and normal general condition**. Laryngoscopy shows an "**omega shaped epiglottis**, short AE fold, redundant mucosa over arytenoids, signs of GERD" — and `entqb-thr5-258` keys **direct laryngoscopy** as the confirming test.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Most of these babies need reassurance and antireflux measures, not surgery; `L12` reserves supraglottoplasty for "severe stridor, **cyanotic attacks**, **weight loss, failure to thrive**, chest deformity, **pulmonary hypertension**, hypoxia, hypercarbia". Cross-reference: `entqb-thr5-252`, `entqb-thr8-404`, `entqb-thr5-258`, `enthd-thr-648`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Laryngomalacia is the commonest congenital LARYNGEAL cause of stridor (60 %), giving inspiratory stridor from a few weeks of age that resolves by 18–24 months; vascular ring is the commonest EXTRAlaryngeal cause.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.164 (Throat ch.7, Stridor & tracheostomy — the chapter heading prints mid-page immediately above this question, which is the chapter’s first; filed under `ent-paedlar` by content). Incidence figures, the descending-order anomaly list, the clinical course and the surgical indications quoted verbatim from `L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf`; the second incidence statement, the 60 % figure, the 2:1 sex ratio, the subglottic-stenosis ranking and the extralaryngeal list from `L6) Stridor  and tracheostomy.pdf`. **⚠️ MIRROR PAIR WITH THE BANK’S OWN Q650 (most common EXTRAlaryngeal cause → vascular ring) over an IDENTICAL option block; Q650 is outside this batch. DO NOT MERGE.** Cross-reference: `entqb-thr5-252`, `entqb-thr8-404`, `entqb-thr5-258`, `enthd-thr-648`.' }
